      "location": "Copenhagen",
      "gift": "1885-1962",
      "summary": "The Danish physicist who married the atom to the quantum and made Copenhagen the capital of physics. In 1913 Bohr took Rutherford's nuclear atom and added the rule that electrons may only occupy fixed orbits, jumping between them only in discrete quanta — emitting or absorbing light of exactly the right colour. It explained the hydrogen spectrum so precisely that the model simply had to be true.\n\nBohr then spent four decades as the field's conscience: the founder of the \"Copenhagen interpretation\" of quantum mechanics, the teacher of Heisenberg, and Einstein's courteous opponent in a twenty-year debate about whether the universe had given up being sensible. He is remembered for complementarity — the idea that light is both wave and particle, and that you may use each picture but not both at once.\n",
