      "location": "Manchester",
      "gift": "1871-1937",
      "summary": "The New Zealander who found the atom's blank centre. Working in Manchester in 1909, Rutherford directed alpha particles at a sheet of gold foil and discovered that about one in eight thousand bounced back — an impossibility in the plum-pudding atom J. J. Thomson had proposed. \"It was almost as incredible as if you fired a 15-inch shell at a piece of tissue paper and it came back and hit you,\" he said.\n\nFrom that result came the nuclear atom: almost all the mass and all the positive charge packed into a point at the centre, with the electrons swimming in near-empty space around it. Rutherford went on to split the atom in 1919 and to preside over the Cavendish Laboratory as its greatest cheerleader, supervising the generation that discovered the neutron and cracked the nucleus.",
