      "location": "Berlin",
      "gift": "1879-1955",
      "summary": "The most famous scientist of the twentieth century, and the man who rebuilt physics twice. In 1905, working as a patent clerk in Bern, Einstein published four papers that rearranged the discipline: the photoelectric effect (for which he would win the 1921 Nobel Prize), Brownian motion, the special theory of relativity, and the equivalence of mass and energy — E = mc².\n\nTen years later he pushed on to the general theory of relativity, recasting gravity not as a force but as the curvature of spacetime itself. It was Einstein who, with a simple thought experiment about a man falling from a roof, decided that gravity and acceleration must be the same thing — and it was Einstein who spent the rest of his life resisting the quantum mechanics his own work had helped create.\n",
