      "sim": {
        "id": "blackbody",
        "label": "Simulation · the blackbody spectrum",
        "caption": "Heat a furnace and the glow follows one curve — the one Planck had to quantise to draw.",
        "vector": {
          "time": 0.8,
          "history": 0.7,
          "calendar": 0.6
        }
      },
      "story": "In October 1911, in the unlikely luxury of the Hotel Métropole in Brussels, the men and the one woman who ran the physics of their time gathered to argue about the newest subject in science: whether matter and energy are continuous after all, or grainy at the bottom. The conference had been paid for by Ernest Solvay, a Belgian chemist who had made a fortune in ammonia and chosen to spend some of it on fundamental physics, and it was the first international congress ever devoted to a single physical question — the quantum. Max Planck, who had lit the fuse, was there; so were Marie Curie, Henri Poincaré, Albert Einstein, and a young Ernest Rutherford who wrote home to Marie in Manchester that he had found the meeting very good, with fair minds gathered together.\n\nThe star of the show turned out to be the most junior person in the room. Einstein had come not as a revolutionary but as a doubter: he was in the middle of a campaign for a different quantum law — the law, he argued, under which the specific heats of solids would drop at low temperatures — and he lectured hard against Planck's account of the black-body law. Poincaré listened, went home, and did the first serious mathematical study of the problem; within months he had produced a famous memoir arguing that the quantum was unavoidable, and nearly derailing Planck's whole picture in the process. It was the first of many times that Solvay (and its quarrels) pushed the field forward.\n\nThe 1911 meeting is the ancestor of every physics conference since: small, international, single-topic, paid for by money from outside the university system, run in the shadow of an emperor's spa town. It produced the same confrontation-between-generations that would characterise the meetings of 1927 and 1930, when Bohr and Einstein would hold their great arguments over determinism. Twenty-nine names appear on the famous photograph — a 'painting in physics,' as some like to say. What attracted all of them, in the end, was less the agenda than the chance to stare at each other's results and argue in person about the shape of the coming century.",
      "significance": "The Solvay Conference of October 1911 did two things. It put the quantum at the centre of the international agenda — Poincaré's subsequent memoir proved to even the most reluctant continental physicist that the discontinuity was here to stay, and Einstein's specific-heats work gave Planck's hypothesis exact experimental tests far from the black body. And it created the institutional form by which physics would be steered for the next half-century: elite, invitation-only, single-theme meetings at which the leading figures settled (or failed to settle) the terms of the field.\n\nIt is no accident that the conference is best remembered for its successors. The 1927 Solvay, with its famous photograph and its Bohr-Einstein debate, is the moment the quantum picture of reality was publicly fought out; the 1930 and 1933 meetings carried the fallout of that debate. In 1911 the seed was planted. When the field's own historian, Léon Rosenfeld, called Solvay 'the perfect instrument' for the progress of physics, he was describing what this first, hurried, sweltering meeting in a hotel lobby had created.",
