      "gift": "1643-1727",
      "summary": "The English natural philosopher who gave physics its laws. In his annus mirabilis of plague years, 1665–66, a young Cambridge student invented the calculus, the generalised binomial theorem, and the mathematics of light — then sat on most of it. The Principia of 1687 turned the cosmos into a problem of inverse-square gravitation: the same force that drops an apple holds the Moon on its path and the planets on theirs.\n\nNewton's three laws of motion and his law of universal gravitation stood, with only Einstein's corrections, for more than two centuries. He is buried in Westminster Abbey; Pope's couplet said it: \"Nature and Nature's laws lay hid in night: God said, Let Newton be! and all was light.\"\n",
