      "summary": "The naturalist who gave biology its organising law. From five years aboard HMS Beagle, and two decades of slow doubt, Darwin delivered On the Origin of Species in 1859: species change, the engine is natural selection — those variants best fitted to their circumstances survive and pass the trait on — and all life descends from common ancestors.\n\nHis theory did what no creation account could: it explained the fit of organism to environment without a designer. The Origin was published jointly with Alfred Russel Wallace, who had reached the same conclusion independently. Darwin died in 1882 at Down House, and was buried in Westminster Abbey beside Newton.\n",
