      "summary": "The only person ever to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences. Marie Skłodowska Curie, born in Warsaw, moved to Paris in 1891, married Pierre Curie, and in 1898 discovered two new elements — polonium and radium — while studying the mysterious rays Henri Becquerel had found in uranium. She named the phenomenon radioactivity.\n\nHer research was carried on with almost no equipment in a leaky shed, her notebooks remain radioactive to this day. She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize, won the Physics prize in 1903 and the Chemistry prize in 1911, and died in 1934 from aplastic anaemia almost certainly caused by her exposure to radiation.\n",
