      "id": "auditing",
      "type": "concept",
      "label": "Auditing",
      "tag": "Accounting",
      "dates": [
        1854
      ],
      "location": "The accountant's room",
      "summary": "The profession of checking other people's books, born when limited liability made investors dependent on numbers they could not verify. The auditor's signature is worth money precisely because it is hard to earn.",
      "vector": {
        "audit": 1,
        "accounting": 0.9,
        "fraud": 0.8,
        "risk": 0.7,
        "firm": 0.8,
        "standards": 0.7,
        "profit": 0.4
      },
      "community": "the ledger",
      "related": [
        {
          "to": "accounting-standards",
          "kind": "applied"
        },
        {
          "to": "enron-collapse",
          "kind": "uncovered"
        },
        {
          "to": "henri-girard",
          "kind": "documented"
        }
      ],
      "story": "The word 'audit' comes from the Latin audire, to hear - a relic of the ancient Roman practice of reading accounts aloud so that officials could be held to their numbers. The modern profession, though, is the child of the limited liability company. In the early nineteenth century a shareholder who suspected his company was hiding something had almost no way to find out; the books were the managers' private property. Britain's Companies Act of 1844 changed that, requiring companies to be audited, and the Limited Liability Act of 1855 gave investors the one thing that made the whole system work - protection from the company's debts. From that moment, strangers were lending money and trusting numbers they could not check themselves, and the profession of the independent auditor was born.\n\nIt grew up where the money was. The first professional bodies - the Society of Accountants in Edinburgh in 1854, the Institute of Chartered Accountants in England and Wales in 1880 - turned accounting and auditing into a chartered trade with examinations and ethics. In the United States the profession developed differently, and the crash of 1929 gave it its great public role: the securities laws of 1933-34 made an audited annual report a legal requirement for every listed company, and the auditor became a permanent fixture of the American capital market. For a century the auditor's job was understood as checking the annual accounts and signing an opinion on them.\n\nThen came Enron. Arthur Andersen, one of the 'Big Five' accounting firms, had been Enron's auditor for years, collecting enormous fees for consulting as well as auditing - and signed off on statements that were, in substance, fiction. When the truth emerged in late 2001, Andersen was convicted of destroying documents and collapsed; the scandal consumed the profession. The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 created the Public Company Accounting Oversight Board (PCAOB) to supervise auditors, forced audit committees to be truly independent, and made the chief executive personally sign the accounts. The auditor's world was rebuilt around the single insight of the whole history of the profession: an audit is only worth money if the auditor is truly independent.",
      "mechanism": "What an auditor actually does is collect evidence that the financial statements are not materially misleading. The work is disciplined scepticism. The auditor vouches: takes entries in the accounts and traces them back to the source documents - the invoice, the contract, the bank statement. The auditor confirms: writes to the company's banks and customers asking them to verify the balances. The auditor tests: rather than checking every one of a million invoices, the auditor samples a representative slice and, if the slice is clean, accepts the whole with statistical confidence. The auditor observes: counts the inventory in the warehouse rather than taking management's word. And the auditor analyses: compares this year's ratios to last year's and to the industry's, and goes looking for anything that does not behave as expected.\n\nAt the end comes the opinion, the famous signature. If the evidence holds up, the auditor issues an unqualified 'clean' opinion: the statements present fairly, in all material respects, the financial position of the company. If there are problems, the opinion is qualified, adverse, or a disclaimer. The crucial word is 'material': an audit does not guarantee that every penny is correct, only that no error big enough to change an investor's decision has escaped. And crucially, an audit is not a fraud investigation - it is designed to give reasonable assurance that the accounts are not materially wrong, not to catch a determined conspiracy.\n\nThat distinction - the audit expectation gap - is the profession's recurring trouble. The public expects a bloodhound; the law, in effect, expects a watchdog. Auditors rely on internal controls, on the assumption that most people are honest, and on sampling; a determined management can beat an audit and still receive a clean opinion. The modern reforms, born of Enron, push back in one direction only: making the auditor so independent, so supervised, and so personally exposed that the temptation to go along is removed.",
      "significance": "The auditor's signature is worth money precisely because it is hard to earn. The entire architecture of modern capital markets - strangers lending, investing, and trading on published numbers - rests on the assumption that those numbers have been checked by someone whose interest is the truth. The audit is the guarantee that makes the financial statement useful: without it, every investor would have to hire their own private investigator, and the market would be much smaller, much slower, and much more nervous.\n\nThe collapse of Enron and Arthur Andersen showed the guarantee failing, and the aftermath rebuilt it. The Public Company Accounting Oversight Board now inspects audit firms and can discipline them; audit partners rotate; audit committees must be genuinely independent of management; and the auditor is barred from selling the kind of consulting that turned Andersen into Enron's business partner. The Sarbanes-Oxley Act, signed in July 2002, is the profession's Magna Carta and its confession all at once - a document that exists only because the old system failed.\n\n'The integrity of information must take priority over a desire for cost efficiencies or competitive advantage in the audit process,' the SEC chairman Arthur Levitt told the profession in 1998, three years before Enron proved him right. That sentence is the whole history of auditing in a nutshell: a profession that began as a way of making limited liability safe for investors, that grew rich by serving the companies it was supposed to check, and that had to be dragged back, by scandal and statute, to the thing it is actually for - telling the truth about other people's books.",
