      "gift": "1447-1517",
      "summary": "The Franciscan friar who wrote down double-entry bookkeeping, and so taught Europe to count its own honesty. In 1494 Pacioli published Summa de Arithmetica, a compendium of mathematics that included a Venetian manual of the ledger: every debit to one account is a credit to another, and every entry is recorded twice, so the books must always balance.\n\nPacioli did not invent double entry — merchants had been keeping it for a century — but he was the first to print it, and printing made it law. He called the ledger \"the eye of commerce\", and he gave the world the trial balance by which a business proves to itself, every month, that it has not lost track.\n",
