      "clipping": {
        "newspaper": "The Houston Chronicle",
        "date": "2001",
        "headline": "A COMPANY THAT INVENTED ITS OWN REVENUE",
        "body": "Federal investigators are examining an energy firm whose accounts appear to have depended on partnerships invented, it is alleged, to make losses vanish. The phrase ‘mark-to-market’ is being explained to juries in simpler language. Shareholders who asked how the books balanced were shown the books."
      },
      "story": "In the 1990s Enron was the most admired company in America. Built in 1985 from the merger of Houston Natural Gas and InterNorth, it transformed itself from a pipeline company into a gas and power trading house, and then into a supposedly universal trading machine - energy, water, broadband, weather derivatives. Fortune named it America's most innovative company five years running, from 1996 to 2001. Its executives were celebrated as the smartest guys in the room, and its stock rose from a split-adjusted price of a few dollars to a peak of more than ninety. The transformation was made possible by two accounting techniques that would become infamous: mark-to-market accounting, which let Enron book the expected profit of long contracts the day they were signed, and a forest of off-balance-sheet partnerships.\n\nThere were warning signs, ignored or shouted down. In March 2001 the Fortune reporter Bethany McLean published 'Is Enron Overpriced?', which asked, on the basis of public filings, exactly how the company made its money; McLean could not get an answer. The short-seller Jim Chanos also read the filings and began betting against the stock. In April 2001, on a conference call, the chief executive Jeff Skilling insulted an analyst who had the temerity to ask where the balance sheet was. Skilling resigned in August 2001, citing personal reasons, and the founder Kenneth Lay took back the job. In October 2001 Enron announced a third-quarter loss of $618 million and a $1.2 billion reduction in shareholder equity - the result, it emerged, of the very partnerships it had so vigorously defended.\n\nThe collapse was sudden and complete. The SEC opened an inquiry; the credit rating agencies cut Enron to junk; a rescue takeover by the rival Dynegy fell apart; and on December 2, 2001, Enron filed for bankruptcy - the largest in American history at the time, destroying more than sixty billion dollars of market value and, with it, the retirement savings of thousands of employees whose 401(k) accounts had been frozen as the stock fell. The auditor, Arthur Andersen, was indicted and convicted of destroying documents, and the firm - one of the world's five largest - collapsed. Kenneth Lay and Jeff Skilling were convicted of fraud in 2006; Lay died of a heart attack before sentencing, and Skilling was sentenced to twenty-four years in prison. Out of the wreckage came the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002, the most sweeping reform of corporate governance and auditing since the 1930s - which is why this node, like the modern audit rulebook itself, exists.",
      "mechanism": "The mechanics of Enron's fraud were, at bottom, the mechanics of hiding losses and inventing profits. The first engine was mark-to-market accounting. Enron, as a trader, argued that its contracts should be valued at what the market would pay for them today. The problem was that many of its contracts - a twenty-year power deal, a broadband network - had no market at all, so Enron's own models supplied the value, and the models were optimistic in the direction of the people who built them. Revenue and profit could be booked years before any cash arrived.\n\nThe second engine was the special-purpose entity. Chief financial officer Andrew Fastow created a family of partnerships - LJM, named after his children, and the Raptors - which borrowed money from banks and used it to buy Enron's assets, including the bad ones. Because the partnerships were legally separate, Enron could remove liabilities and losses from its own balance sheet. But the structure was not what it appeared: the partnerships were guaranteed by Enron's own stock, and Enron was expected to make good on their obligations. As long as the stock rose, the illusion held. When it began to fall in 2001, the guarantees turned from paper into demands for real cash, and the hidden losses came home all at once.\n\nThe third engine was round-trip trading: Enron and certain trading partners sold power to each other at inflated prices, both booking revenue as if real value had changed hands, when in fact it was a swap of cash and profit with no net economic effect. Each of these techniques individually was defended as legitimate business; together they produced an income statement that bore little relation to the company's actual economics. The real mechanism of the fraud, in the end, was the gap between what the statements showed and what the business did - a gap that the auditor, Arthur Andersen, was paid eleven years running not to see.",
      "significance": "Enron is the reason the modern audit rulebook exists. Before December 2001, the public had accepted the audited financial statement as a fairly reliable picture of a company. Enron demonstrated, on the largest possible stage, that audited numbers could be fiction, and the consequence was the most sweeping regulatory response since the 1930s. The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 created the Public Company Accounting Oversight Board to supervise auditors, forced chief executives and chief financial officers to certify the accounts personally, required audit committees to be genuinely independent, and barred auditors from the consulting work that had made Andersen Enron's partner rather than its check.\n\nIts significance goes beyond the statute. Enron is taught in every business school as the case study in how accounting rules can be gamed, in the meaning of mark-to-market and special-purpose entities, and in the difference between revenue and cash. It gave the world the phrase 'the smartest guys in the room' as a warning rather than a compliment. It demonstrated, painfully, that the credibility of markets rests on trust in numbers - the same point the SEC chairman Arthur Levitt had made three years earlier when he warned of an erosion in the quality of earnings. It also gave birth to the modern profession of forensic accounting, whose practitioners reconstruct what the books should have said when the auditors did not.\n\nFor the hundreds of institutions and thousands of employees who lost money, the significance is personal rather than doctrinal. Enron's fall taught a generation of investors that 'the business is sound' is a sentence that has to be checked, and that the language of financial statements - for all its precision - is spoken by people with incentives. Every reform that followed, from auditor rotation to whistle-blower bounties, is an attempt to answer the question the company's collapse posed: how much can the numbers be trusted, and how can we make them more trustworthy?",
