      "story": "Macroeconomics was born of a failure. For most of the nineteenth century, economists studied the parts, prices, wages, rents, the behaviour of a single market, and assumed that if each part behaved sensibly, the whole would take care of itself. This is the assumption the Great Depression broke. From 1929 to 1933 American output fell by nearly a third, unemployment rose to about a quarter of the labour force, and markets failed to heal themselves: prices fell, wages fell, and still the economy did not recover. The invisible hand, it turned out, was holding a small blade.\n\nThe discipline's founding text arrived in 1936 with John Maynard Keynes's The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money, which argued that the economy is not a collection of independent parts but a system, and that its key variable is aggregate demand, total spending. If spending collapses, output and employment collapse with it, and no amount of falling wages restores balance, because falling wages mean falling incomes, which mean falling spending. The whole, Keynes insisted, can be sick even when every part is behaving rationally. The word macroeconomics itself was coined in the early 1930s by Ragnar Frisch, the Norwegian economist who also founded the journal Econometrica, to name the study of the economy in the aggregate.\n\nThe Depression also created the tools of the trade: national income accounting, the measurement of GDP, was developed in the 1930s and 1940s by Simon Kuznets and others, precisely so that governments could see the size of the patient they were trying to heal. And the field has grown by debate ever since. Keynes answered the classical idea that markets self-correct; Milton Friedman's monetarism answered Keynes; the two still frame every argument about what governments and central banks should do, and the two-percent inflation target of modern central banks is one of macroeconomics' few genuine consensuses.",
      "mechanism": "The mechanism of macroeconomics is the circular flow of income. Picture money moving in a loop: households earn wages by selling labour to firms, and spend those wages buying the goods firms produce. The money that households spend becomes the revenue that firms use to pay wages and profits, which become household income again. As long as the loop is intact, spending equals income equals production. Macroeconomics is, at bottom, the study of what happens to that loop and what breaks it.\n\nThe break, in Keynes's account, happens when households or firms decide to spend less. Suppose everyone, frightened by bad news, saves a little more. Saving is money withdrawn from the loop. Firms find their sales falling, so they produce less and lay people off; the laid-off earn less, so they spend less; and the decline feeds on itself. This is the paradox of thrift: what is prudent for each family is ruinous for the whole. Output falls until incomes have fallen far enough that the extra saving is matched by something, investment, government spending, exports, that puts money back into the loop. If nothing does, the economy settles at a depressed equilibrium with high unemployment, which is precisely what happened after 1929.\n\nMacroeconomics therefore treats the national economy as a system with a few big dials: total spending, total employment, the price level, and the interest rate set by the central bank. The interest rate is the master dial, because it controls how much it costs to borrow and how much it pays to save, the two decisions that determine whether money flows back into the loop or stays out of it. The discipline's job is to keep the loop spinning at full speed without letting it spin so fast that inflation takes off.",
      "significance": "Macroeconomics matters because it is the theory of how governments and central banks steer the economy, and its failures have had world-historical consequences. The Depression produced Keynes; the double-digit inflation of the 1970s produced monetarism; the 2008 financial crisis produced a scramble of old remedies and new doubts. Each generation discovers that the system of loops and dials is more complicated than the last model said, and that policy made in one era can look reckless in the next.\n\nIts greatest practical contribution may be the most mundane: it made the health of the national economy a visible, measurable object of policy. Before GDP was measured, a depression was a rumour; after it was measured, it became a number that governments were held responsible for. That is why macroeconomics is taught to every economics student, and why it connects to every other node in this library: inflation is its price-level dial, money and banking its plumbing, Keynesian economics its most famous doctrine, and the crash of 1929 the wound it was invented to dress.",
