      "summary": "The moral philosopher who founded economics as a discipline. In 1776 Smith published An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations, arguing that exchange, specialisation and self-interest, left to organise themselves, coordinate entire economies without a planner. The pin factory, the invisible hand, and the division of labour all come from this book.\n\nSmith was first a moralist — The Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759) built sympathy into human nature — and the wealth book is inseparable from that ethics. He was no champion of greed: his point was that a society of free exchange lets each man's pursuit of his own interest serve the interest of everyone else, \"as if by an invisible hand\".\n",
