      "story": "Adam Smith was born in 1723 in Kirkcaldy, a small Scottish port on the Firth of Forth, the only son of a customs official who died before the boy was born. At fourteen he went up to the University of Glasgow, where he sat at the feet of Francis Hutcheson, the moral philosopher whose lectures left on Smith the permanent mark of the Scottish Enlightenment: that the study of man is a study of his social nature. In 1740 he won a scholarship to Balliol College, Oxford, where he was bored and unimpressed - the English universities taught little and their tutors were paid regardless - and he returned to Scotland to lecture on rhetoric and jurisprudence in Edinburgh, earning just enough to keep his dignity.\n\nIn 1751 Glasgow appointed him professor of logic, and the next year professor of moral philosophy, the chair he held for more than a decade. His lectures formed the basis of his first book, The Theory of Moral Sentiments, published in 1759, which made his name and gave him his central moral idea: human beings are equipped with sympathy, the capacity to enter into one another's feelings, and it is this, not reason or self-love, that holds society together. The book is often read as a different Smith from the one who wrote the Wealth of Nations, but Smith himself saw no contradiction - the one described how we judge each other, the other how we trade with each other.\n\nIn 1764 Smith resigned his chair to travel as tutor to the young Duke of Buccleuch, a post that took him through France and Switzerland for two years and paid him a lifetime pension. In Paris he met the physiocrats - Quesnay and Turgot - who argued that wealth flowed from the land and that the state should let it flow freely; the meetings sharpened his own thinking, though he never accepted the physiocratic doctrine. Back in Kirkcaldy he settled down to write, and in 1776 he published An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations, a book of nearly a thousand pages that began with the division of labour and ended with a calm dismantling of the mercantile system that had ruled European commerce for a century.\n\nIn 1778 Smith was appointed Commissioner of Customs in Edinburgh, a post he held for the rest of his life - the great critic of tariffs spending his days administering them, an irony he bore with equanimity and, by most accounts, with competence. He died in Edinburgh in 1790, and, in a last act characteristic of the man, ordered his executors to burn his unpublished manuscripts. The Wealth of Nations had made him the father of political economy; the Theory of Moral Sentiments had made him, in his own mind, the moralist he had first set out to be.",
      "mechanism": "The Wealth of Nations rests on a small machinery that Smith sets out in the first chapters and never lets go. The engine is the division of labour: instead of each household making everything it needs, each worker specialises in one task, and specialisation multiplies output enormously. Smith's example is the pin factory, where ten workers, each doing a single step, could make tens of thousands of pins a day, while one man working alone, doing every step himself, could struggle to make even one. The division of labour is limited by the extent of the market: a village cannot support a full-time watchmaker, a great city can.\n\nWhat makes the divided workers cooperate without a director is exchange, and what makes exchange possible is the human disposition to truck and barter. Each worker produces more of his speciality than he needs and trades the surplus; prices coordinate the whole. Smith then asks how prices are set. In the short run, the market price wanders up and down with supply and demand, but beneath it works what he calls the natural price - the level that just covers rent, wages, and profit. When the market price rises above the natural price, more producers are attracted in, output grows, and the price is pressed back down; when it falls below, producers leave. The famous invisible hand is the summary of all this: a merchant, pursuing only his own gain, employs capital and labour in ways that, unplanned, serve the public good - because the whole system of prices channels self-interest into service without anyone intending it.",
      "significance": "Smith's significance is almost too large to state soberly: he effectively founded economics as a field of study. Before 1776, thinking about wealth was scattered across merchant manuals, tracts on coinage, and the doctrine of what came to be called mercantilism - the belief that a nation's wealth lay in its hoard of gold, and that trade was a zero-sum contest to be won by tariffs and monopolies. The Wealth of Nations demolished that view with the argument that wealth lies in the annual produce of land and labour - what a country can actually make and sell - and that the market, left free, is a better distributor of resources than any minister of state.\n\nThe book shaped everything that followed. Ricardo read it and built the theory of rent on it; Marx read it and took from it both the labour theory of value and a lifelong opponent; every free-trade argument of the nineteenth century - the repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846 was very much Smith's victory - drew on it. It is also the source of the discipline's most contested metaphor, the invisible hand, invoked for two centuries both as the banner of laissez-faire and, by its critics, as the emblem of an assumption that self-interest always serves the public. Smith himself never drew that conclusion. In the very book that trusted the market he also wrote that 'no society can surely be flourishing and happy, of which the far greater part of the members are poor and miserable.' The man who trusted the market never stopped worrying about the poor.",
