      "summary": "The economist who decided, in the worst depression the world had seen, that the government must step in. Keynes's General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936) argued that demand, not supply, drives output: when households and firms lose confidence and stop spending, the economy can settle at an equilibrium of high unemployment — and only the state can spend it back to life.\n\nThe Great Depression had no textbook answer until Keynes wrote one, and the postwar order — full employment policy, stabilisation, the toolbox of fiscal policy — was built on his book. He was also a Bloomsbury intellectual, a government adviser at Versailles and Bretton Woods, a speculator, and the economist who quipped that in the long run we are all dead.\n",
