      "clipping": {
        "newspaper": "The New York Herald Tribune",
        "date": "29 October 1929",
        "headline": "THE TICKER FALLS SILENT AND THE BOOM IS UNDONE",
        "body": "More than sixteen million shares changed hands yesterday and the ticker ran four hours behind the trading floor. What the papers call a correction is being called, on the curb outside, something else. The margin calls have begun arriving by telephone."
      },
      "story": "The American stock market spent the 1920s climbing a staircase of pure confidence. The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose almost without interruption from 1921 to 1929, and by the middle of 1929 it had more than doubled in four years. The belief that stocks could only go up was its own engine: brokers' loans, the money lent by banks to buyers on margin, reached eight billion dollars, and the margin requirement meant a buyer with ten thousand dollars could control fifty thousand dollars of stock. Irving Fisher, the most celebrated economist in America, told the world in October 1929 that stock prices had reached what looked like a permanently high plateau.\n\nThe plateau lasted four days. On 24 October, Black Thursday, nearly thirteen million shares changed hands and the ticker tape ran hours behind the trading floor. The panic was staved off briefly by a pool of bankers, but nothing held. On 29 October, Black Tuesday, sixteen million shares sold, many at prices far below the last quote, and the ticker, the era's symbol of perfect information, fell silent for hours. The market lost roughly a quarter of its value in two weeks, and the crash was not the bottom but the first step of a stair that would descend for three years. By July 1932 the Dow had lost almost ninety percent of its 1929 peak, and the whole construction of borrowed confidence had been erased.\n\nThe crash did not cause the Depression alone. Economists have argued for a century over how much of the collapse of output and employment was due to the stock market and how much to monetary contraction, trade wars, and bank failures. But it is the image every later crash is compared to, the standard by which a selloff is measured and the event that made the connection between a speculation and a depression visible to everyone. Franklin Roosevelt, four years later, stood in his first inaugural address and told the country that the only thing we have to fear is fear itself, a sentence that read differently after Black Tuesday than it could have in the summer of 1929.",
      "mechanism": "The mechanism of the crash was leverage running in reverse. Buyers on margin had borrowed most of the purchase price of their stock; when prices began to fall, the brokers issued margin calls, demanding that buyers put up more cash or have their stock sold to cover the loans. Because so many buyers had no cash, the margin call forced sale, the forced sale pushed prices down further, and the further fall triggered more margin calls on stocks bought at higher prices. The accelerator loop, price down, call, sale, price down, ran for days, and it converted a valuation problem into a liquidity emergency. The same leverage that had magnified gains in the boom exactly magnified losses in the bust.\n\nThe second mechanism was contagion through the banking system. Brokers' loans had come from the banks, and when the stock loans soured and stock prices collapsed, the banks' asset values collapsed with them. Runs on banks, which had already been spreading through the agricultural Midwest, began in earnest in 1930, and bank failures destroyed the money supply itself: when a bank fails, the deposits it promised are erased, and the whole economy's means of payment shrinks. Irving Fisher, who had endorsed the plateau, spent the next decade studying what he called debt deflation, the theory that falling prices make debts heavier in real terms, that debtors default, that banks fail, and that the deflation feeds itself.\n\nIt is this second stage that ties the crash to the Depression. A stock market crash could have been a correction; what turned it into catastrophe was the interaction of falling asset prices with a banking system holding those assets, a monetary authority that watched passively, and a wave of bankruptcies. The economists who later studied it, Galbraith, Kindleberger, and the monetarist Friedman, agreed on the anatomy even where they disagreed on the prescription: leverage plus panic plus fragile banks equals a crash that is not a clearing of the air but a hole in the floor.",
      "significance": "The crash of 1929 is the benchmark event in the history of financial markets, the image every later crash is compared to. Its significance is partly moral: it ended the assumption that stock prices are set by fundamentals rather than by what the crowd believes, and it installed the crash and the ensuing Depression at the centre of the twentieth century's political argument about whether markets can regulate themselves. The decade after it saw the New Deal, the Securities and Exchange Commission, and the Glass-Steagall separation of commercial and investment banking, all attempts to discipline the leverage the crash had exposed.\n\nFor economics, the significance is theoretical. The crash did not cause the Depression alone, but it revealed the mechanisms, leverage, margin calls, bank runs, debt deflation, by which a financial panic becomes an economic depression. Fisher's debt deflation, Friedman and Schwartz's monetary interpretation, and the model of financial fragility built later by Kindleberger and Minsky all descend from the events of October 1929. The event remains the standard by which a selloff is measured, and the warning that when the ticker falls silent, the boom is undone.",
