      "story": "Every technology waits for its moment, and the transistor's moment arrived in the winter of 1947 in a rain-and-snow smeared laboratory at Bell Telephone Laboratories in Murray Hill, New Jersey. Bell's research director Mervin Kelly had spent the war seeing vacuum tubes fail in telephone switching systems and had decided that crystals - semiconductors - might replace them. He hired the theorist John Bardeen, whose explanation of why William Shockley's field-effect idea kept failing pointed the way, and paired him with the ingenious experimenter Walter Brattain, who could get blood from a stone and an amplifier from a slice of germanium.\n\nOn December 16, 1947, Brattain pressed a wedge carrying two gold contacts into a slab of high-purity germanium and found he could modulate the current through one contact with voltage on the other, amplifying a signal about a hundredfold. A week later, on December 23, the team demonstrated the device to Bell executives, who heard amplified speech through headphones. Shockley, who had been elsewhere when the breakthrough happened and never forgave himself for it, called it \"a magnificent Christmas present.\" Bell Labs kept the invention a secret while patents were filed and announced it only on June 30, 1948 - a story the New York Times ran beneath the radio news, so unimpressed was the world.\n\nShockley, stung at having no direct hand in the discovery, locked himself away that January and conceived the junction transistor, a cleaner and mass-producible device. The three men shared the 1956 Nobel Prize in Physics, though their alliance had long since curdled into litigation and rivalry. Shockley moved to California and founded the company that seeded Silicon Valley; the employees who fled him began Fairchild, and from Fairchild grew Intel and the semiconductor industry. From that first half-inch contraption of gold foil and germanium, a technology was born whose descendants now outnumber the stars in our galaxy and, by most counts, by a very wide margin.",
      "mechanism": "A transistor is a semiconductor sandwich with a tap. The semiconductor sits between a conductor and an insulator: its electrical behaviour can be tuned by impurities added in a process called doping. Dope silicon with atoms bearing extra electrons and you get n-type material, rich in negative charge; dope it with atoms that leave holes and you get p-type, rich in positive vacancies. Lay these regions together and junctions form that conduct electricity in one direction and not the other.\n\nThe genius of the transistor is that a small signal can steer a large one. In a bipolar transistor, a sprinkling of current entering the base region lowers the energetic barrier in the junction, letting a comparatively large current flood from emitter to collector. The input is a gossip's whisper; the output is the whole conversation made loud. In a field-effect transistor, the commonest kind in a modern chip, a metal gate sits on top of the conducting channel, separated by a thin insulating layer, and a voltage on the gate - not a current, so almost no power is spent - either attracts charges into the channel or repels them, turning the channel on or off.\n\nThat on-or-off behaviour is what silicon hardware is built on. Let the transistor saturate and it is a closed switch; cut it off and it is an open switch, exactly the two states a bit needs. Wiring thousands of these switches together reproduces the logic gates of Boole and Shannon, and because the gate draws almost no current, millions of transistors can switch billions of times a second inside a chip that still fits in the palm of your hand and is barely warm.",
      "significance": "The transistor is the invention that made the information age physically possible. It replaced the vacuum tube - a device that was bulky, fragile, power-hungry, and hot - with a sliver of solid material that costs virtually nothing to manufacture and almost nothing to run. ENIAC needed a thirty-ton room of tubes to do arithmetic; the phone in your pocket does it faster with switches you could not see with a microscope. The transistor is the link that turns the logic gate from a curiosity into a civilization.\n\nIts economics shaped the age as much as its physics. Bell Labs licensed the transistor widely, and the semiconductor industry refined it along the curve Gordon Moore codified in 1965: the number of transistors on a chip doubling every couple of years. That prediction, self-fulfilling because an industry chose to obey it, is why computing kept getting cheaper while it got faster, which is why the computer stopped being a government machine and became a consumer purchase. The transistor also settled a philosophical point, silently: the machine that could imitate thought (see the-thinking-machine, artificial-intelligence) turned out to be built from billions of the same humble switch, which suggests the wonders of computation are assembled rather than conjured.",
