      "story": "The war in Europe was being fought with tables of numbers. Every new artillery piece demanded firing tables - hundreds of trajectories computed by hand, each trajectory taking up to forty hours of desk-calculator labour at the Army's Ballistic Research Laboratory. The Moore School of Electrical Engineering at the University of Pennsylvania had an analogue differential analyzer that could do a trajectory in about thirty minutes, but it was one machine, and it could not keep up. In 1942 the physicist John Mauchly, who had pondered weather prediction, wrote a five-page memo, \"The Use of Vacuum Tube Devices in Calculating,\" proposing an all-electronic machine that could multiply a thousand times a second.\n\nJ. Presper Eckert, a brilliant young engineer then aged twenty-three, pronounced the idea possible and supplied the engineering nerve. The pair's report persuaded Army Ordnance, and on May 9, 1943 - Eckert's twenty-fourth birthday - the contract was signed. For two and a half years a team of some fifty people built the Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer in a long room on the first floor of the Moore School: forty panels in a U shape, about 17,468 vacuum tubes, 70,000 resistors, 10,000 capacitors, 1500 relays, and several miles of wiring. The machine ran on 140 kilowatts and a 100,000-pulse-per-second clock. Skeptics predicted the tubes would fail by the thousands; in practice the team kept the filaments under-voltage and a tube failed about every two days.\n\nENIAC came too late to fire a single wartime shell. Its first real calculation, run in November and December 1945, was a set of top-secret nuclear problems for Los Alamos - Edward Teller's thermonuclear figures - supervised by Nicholas Metropolis and Stan Frankel. On February 15, 1946, the machine was formally dedicated; the New York Times reported \"Electronic Computer Flashes Answers, May Speed Engineering.\" Programming ENIAC meant physically rewiring it: six women - Kay McNulty, Betty Snyder, Frances Bilas, Marlyn Wescoff, Ruth Lichterman, Jean Jennings - figured out, off blueprints, how to make the machine dance, setting thousands of switches and plugging cables. Their work was too subtle for the press to credit for decades. ENIAC moved to the Aberdeen Proving Ground in 1947 and ran until 1955, the loud, hot, magnificent proof that electronics, not gears, was the future of computation.",
      "mechanism": "ENIAC was a decimal machine with personality. Instead of binary digits it counted in base ten, each digit stored by a ring of ten vacuum tubes, each tube holding one of the ten numerals, so a digit was represented by exactly one tube glowing at a time. Twenty accumulators did addition and subtraction, each a ten-digit register; a high-speed multiplier and a combined divider and square-rooter handled the rest. The whole machine was synchronous, pulsed by a clock signal every ten microseconds, so each basic operation took that long - roughly 5,000 additions a second, a thousand times faster than the relay machines of the day.\n\nWhat made ENIAC programmable at all was a profusion of pluggable wiring. Function tables, set with switches, held constants and even stored small sequences that would now be called instructions; the master programmer unit sequenced the machine through the steps of a problem. To change the program, programmers unplugged the telephone-style cables from their trays and replugged them: they were wiring the machine's brain as well as its data path. Eckert, looking back, called this \"pluggable programming,\" neither hard-wired nor stored-program, and it is precisely the awkward stage between the two. The stored-program computer - the idea that instructions could live in memory like data - was the next step, taken by ENIAC's successor EDVAC and John von Neumann, who saw ENIAC at the Moore School and wrote the report that generalized it.",
      "significance": "ENIAC demonstrated beyond dispute that all-electronic computing was not an act of faith but an engineering fact. Its successors sold the idea; ENIAC was the proof. The machine set the pattern for everything that followed: the separation of arithmetic units, the discipline of a clock, the notion that a single general-purpose electronic machine could attack ballistics today and bomb physics tomorrow. When Eckert and Mauchly left to found their own company and build UNIVAC, the first commercial computer, they took ENIAC's lessons to the marketplace, and the stored-program machine of the late 1940s inherited its mantle both practically and mythologically.\n\nIt also left a social inheritance. The six women who taught themselves to program ENIAC from blueprints invented the first debugging methods - breakpoints, single-stepping - and the term \"bug\" was already in print from a moth trapped in a Harvard relay. For decades their contribution was written out of the story, and restoring it has become part of computing's memory work. ENIAC is the object that makes the rest of this library legible: its rewiring prefigures every compiler (see the-first-compiler), its tube-based gates connect to logic-gates, and its room-sized bulk is the joke at the center of the personal computer - the story of how thirty tons of tube became a box in a living room.",
