      "story": "The word algorithm is nine hundred years old and honors a librarian. Around the year 825 the Persian mathematician Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi, working in Baghdad's House of Wisdom, wrote treatises that introduced Hindu-Arabic numerals to the Islamic world and gave us, in Latinized form, his own name as the word \"algorism,\" later \"algorithm.\" His book on algebra supplied the root of a different word, and his step-by-step method for arithmetic - do this, then this, then this - supplied the shape of the whole modern enterprise.\n\nBut the idea is far older than his name. A recipe for finding the greatest common divisor of two numbers survives in Euclid's Elements from about 300 BC, and it is a fully formed algorithm: subtract, swap, repeat, stop. For two millennia an algorithm was a human performance, something a clerk did with pen and ink, and the interesting questions were about speed and cleverness, not about the meaning of the word. The discipline of studying which recipes are fast and which are impossible is barely ninety years old, and its birth certificate was signed in 1936, in two places at once.\n\nIn 1936 Alan Turing published \"On Computable Numbers, with an Application to the Entscheidungsproblem\" and Kurt Godel's challenge - whether every mathematical statement could be mechanically decided - finally met a precise definition of \"mechanical.\" Turing described a machine with a paper tape, a head that reads and writes symbols and moves left or right, obeying a fixed table of rules. Then he showed the striking thing: there exists a single universal machine that can mimic any other. Anything any machine can compute, the universal machine can compute, given the instructions. Alonzo Church, simultaneously and independently, captured the same idea in the lambda calculus and the notion that these definitions cover every effectively calculable function became the Church-Turing thesis. From that point, algorithms were no longer recipes; they were objects of study with limits, and the question \"how fast?\" could be asked about them as seriously as the question \"can it be done at all?\" The baby discipline of the 1940s and 1950s - Knuth's The Art of Computer Programming, the discovery in 1971 that some problems are NP-complete - matured into the quiet science that stands behind every search, sort, and recommendation the world now runs on.",
      "mechanism": "An algorithm is a finite sequence of unambiguous instructions that takes an input, performs operations, and produces an output, halting in the process. The classic worked example is Euclid's algorithm for the greatest common divisor of two numbers: divide the larger by the smaller, replace the larger with the remainder, repeat until the remainder is zero; the last nonzero remainder is the answer. What matters is that the steps are mechanical - no judgment calls, no appeals to intuition - so that both a person and a machine can follow them and arrive at the same result.\n\nThe central refinement is measuring work. Computer scientists express an algorithm's cost as how the number of steps grows with the size of the input, called its complexity. A linear search through a list of n names takes about n comparisons; a good sort takes about n times log n; some problems are only solvable by trying all 2-to-the-n combinations. Complexity is the vocabulary of the field, and it allows honest comparisons between recipes - which is why the discipline can say a quadratic route is worse than a logarithmic one even before either is run on any particular machine.\n\nThere is one more layer worth naming, because it gives algorithms their philosophical bite. Turing's universal machine and his proof that some problems have no algorithm - the halting problem cannot be decided by any program - establish that computation has real ceilings. Not every question is answerable by following rules, and the rules that do answer questions carry measurable prices. Those two facts, the universality and the limit, are the load-bearing walls of computer science.",
      "significance": "Algorithms are the intellectual content of computing, the part machines merely execute. Hardware and software come and go, but the Euclid of the recipe survives; a good algorithm written for a machine in 1965 is still a good algorithm, only faster. This is why the study of algorithms is the most durable element of the curriculum that produced the modern world's search engines, route finders, schedulers, and recommendation systems - each of which is a fairly small recipe running at planetary scale.\n\nThe discipline also changed the very meaning of \"knowing.\" Since Turing, a claim about thinking (see artificial-intelligence, the-thinking-machine) can be sharpened into a claim about an algorithm running on a machine, and the Church-Turing thesis licenses a working assumption: if something can be calculated at all, some algorithm can calculate it. At the same time, complexity theory injected a healthy queasiness into that optimism - the suspicion, still unproven, that some problems that are easy to verify are hard to solve, the question of P versus NP. An entire field therefore lives in the tension between the universal machine's power and the halting problem's wall, and that tension is one of the deepest ideas the century produced: computability has both a horizon and a frontier.",
