      "story": "For the first decade of computing there was no such thing as a programming language, and the absence is worth sitting with. A machine like ENIAC was programmed by plugging cables and throwing switches; its successor generation was programmed in machine code - the actual bit patterns of its instructions, typed or punched with no intermediate step. To program in these terms was to think entirely in the machine's terms, in zeros and ones and register numbers, an occupation for a disciplined few. The invention of assembly language in the late 1940s was already a revolution: it let a human write mnemonic symbols - ADD, SUB - that a translator called an assembler converted to machine code. But the real dam broke when someone decided the machine should do the translating and the human should say what was wanted.\n\nThat someone was Grace Hopper. Working on the Harvard Mark I during the war and then for the Eckert-Mauchly Computer Corporation on the UNIVAC, Hopper felt the waste of hand-coding. Her A-0 system, demonstrated in 1952, was the first compiler: a program that accepted symbolic statements and generated machine instructions from them. Colleagues thought it suspect - computers were supposed to compute, not program. Hopper's rebuttal was practical to the point of wryness: you have to simplify, and make the machine comfortable to work with. Her later remark that the most dangerous phrase in engineering is \"we've always done it this way\" was autobiography. Her persistence fed FLOW-MATIC, which fed COBOL, the business language of 1959 that still runs a surprising share of the world's banking.\n\nThe 1950s were the language fever years. FORTRAN, delivered by John Backus's IBM team in 1957, made scientific notation nearly literal on the page; LISP, McCarthy's 1958 creation, made programs look like the data they processed; ALGOL, the mathematicians' collaborative effort, gave the field its grammar of block structure, and C, born at Bell Labs in 1972 from the bones of Unix, went on to build most of the world's infrastructure. The family tree since is a turbine of branches - Smalltalk and Simula handing on object orientation, Python arriving in 1991 as a \"batteries included\" teaching language, Java and JavaScript conquering the web. Every one of them descends from the translation decision Hopper made in 1952: that humans speak to machines, not in machine.",
      "mechanism": "A programming language is a formal notation for describing computation, and the trick that makes it work is translation by another program. The human writes in a language with syntax - grammar that can be checked mechanically - and meaning, called semantics. The text of a program is first broken into tokens, then parsed into a tree of grammatical structure, then checked for type consistency; because the notation is formal, this whole pipeline can be done by rules. The compiler's output is machine instructions, or an intermediate form that a final pass turns into instructions. Languages where translation happens more lazily, line by line at run time, are called interpreted, which trades speed for flexibility.\n\nThe abstraction is why languages matter. Assembly language and its low-level kin give the coder close command of the metal - registers, addresses, instructions. High-level languages hide the metal: FORTRAN lets a scientist write x = a + b * c without deciding which register holds what; Python lets a beginner move whole collections with a single expression; SQL lets a banker ask for rows without knowing how data is stored. Each level of abstraction is a leap of trust that the compiler or interpreter will do the bookkeeping, and each leap widens the population that can program.\n\nThe deepest mechanism is the one Hopper installed: the language and the machine part ways. Programs become portable objects that can be translated for any machine, and the language becomes an interface - a contract between human intention and silicon. What a language permits, suggests, and makes difficult shapes the thinking of everyone who writes in it, which is why language design is treated as engineering and why the choice of language is often a philosophical argument wearing work clothes.",
      "significance": "Programming languages are the reason software exists in the plural - an economy, not a miracle. Without translation, every program would be an irreplaceable relic of its machine; with it, the same FORTRAN or C source can be recompiled for a new processor decades later, which is how institutions still run code written before their engineers were born. Language design also settled a social question with enormous consequences: who is allowed to program. Machine code restricted computing to specialists; COBOL opened it to business clerks; Basic and Python opened it to children. Whatever computing's moral story is, it begins with the decision that the machine should do the work of learning to understand human - one of the few instances in history where the least powerful party got to set the terms of the conversation.\n\nIndividually, languages are also theories of how to think. Alan Perlis, first Turing Award laureate, observed that a language that does not change the way you think about programming is not worth knowing, and the claim cuts both ways: languages can liberate, as structured programming did, and they can constrict, as Dijkstra argued COBOL did. Because software now runs almost everything, the dialects humans use to write it are among the most consequential intellectual instruments of the century - and the entire apparatus hangs from one slender precedent, the first compiler, whose author had to ask forgiveness rather than permission.",
