      "story": "The operating system was born of scarcity and impatience. Early computers ran one program at a time, attended by human operators who loaded tapes, flipped switches, and watched as the machine sat idle between jobs. The first operating systems, such as GM-NAA I/O for the IBM 704 in 1955, were batch supervisors: they queued jobs, loaded the next one, and printed results, letting the expensive machine work while the people slept. The immediate payoff was throughput; the profession's founding insight was that someone - a program - had to act as traffic controller, and that this controller would itself be the oldest piece of software in the building.\n\nThe 1960s raised the stakes. At MIT, the Compatible Time-Sharing System (CTSS) of 1961 let several people use one machine simultaneously through teletypes, each getting a slice of CPU time so fine that it felt private. CTSS grew into Multics, a grand, overambitious project to build a utility-grade computing system; Bell Labs invested and then withdrew in 1969, sick of the expense. Out of that withdrawal came the strangest gift in the field's history: Ken Thompson and Dennis Ritchie, left with a surplus PDP-7, built a smaller system they called Unix, named as a pun on Multics. Unix's design - tiny kernel, everything else a process, files and devices treated alike - proved so clean that it conquered the world's laboratories and companies, was rewritten in Ritchie's C to become portable, and fathered BSD, Linux, macOS, and every modern family that matters.\n\nThe commercial world followed a parallel track: CP/M for the first microcomputers, then MS-DOS under the IBM PC, then Windows, which wrapped a graphical face around the same babysitting chores. Meanwhile Linus Torvalds's 1991 hobby kernel (see the-linux-kernel) grew, with thousands of collaborators, into the operating system of the internet itself - the silent servant running most servers, most phones, and a surprising fraction of the machinery between your coffee and your inbox.",
      "mechanism": "An operating system is a supervisor program that owns the hardware and lends it out. The core, the kernel, runs with special privilege and does the jobs no ordinary program may: it schedules processes, deciding which running program gets the CPU next and for how long, switching between them so quickly - a few milliseconds per slice - that a machine with one processor feels like many machines. It manages memory through virtual memory: each process is given the illusion of a huge private address space, implemented by page tables that map those imaginary addresses onto real RAM and, when memory gets tight, onto disk.\n\nThe kernel also provides the file system - a named, hierarchical store of data with permissions - and the device drivers that let programs speak to disks, screens, keyboards, and network cards without knowing their wiring. Programs talk to all of this through system calls, the interface Thompson once praised as the clean idea of Unix: open, close, read, write. When a program wants more memory or wants to print, it issues a system call; the kernel checks the request, does the privileged thing, and hands control back. Above the kernel sit libraries and the shell, the layer humans and scripts actually meet.\n\nEverything else in the operating system is a consequence of these decisions. Timeslicing is fairness, virtual memory is isolation - one process cannot read another's memory without permission - and file permissions are the seed of security. When you open a laptop from sleep, you are watching a scheduler, a dozen device drivers, and a file system negotiate a handshake at lightning speed, which is why the oldest software in the machine is also, paradoxically, the one you notice least: it is good at its job entirely by being invisible.",
      "significance": "Operating systems are the unglamorous scaffolding of the entire digital age, and their design choices ripple into politics, economics, and daily life. They decide who can run what on whose machine, which is why antitrust cases have been fought over operating systems, why the internet's servers are overwhelmingly one kernel, and why the desktops of the 1990s became a battlefield between a proprietary giant and an open-source insurgency. An operating system is not merely software; it is a constitution for the sharing of a scarce resource, and constitutions tend to be contested.\n\nThe operating system also made computing social. Batch processing and time-sharing turned the computer from an instrument for one elite to a shared utility, and Unix's philosophy - small programs composed through pipes, in McIlroy's memorable call for \"some ways of coupling programs like garden hose\" - gave the field its ethic of modularity and fellowship. When the personal computer arrived (see the-personal-computer), it carried an operating system in its belly, and the same supervisory software that had once governed a roomful of scientists now governed the family machine. In an age of billions of always-on devices, the operating system's ancient bargain - total control over the hardware, in exchange for total humility - remains the quiet agreement on which every other piece of software depends.",
