      "story": "The internet was designed to be un-killable, which is rather the point. In the early 1960s, at the height of the Cold War, the RAND Corporation's Paul Baran asked how the military could keep communicating if parts of the network were destroyed. His 1964 report, \"On Distributed Communications,\" proposed a network with no single center: messages chopped into small blocks, each routed independently through redundant channels, reassembled at the far end. A destroyed node would be routed around. Independently, a few hundred miles away at Britain's National Physical Laboratory, Donald Davies was developing the same idea and gave it its present name - packet switching - while the psychologist-cum-researcher J. C. R. Licklider was at ARPA preaching a bold dream of an \"Intergalactic Computer Network\" that would let machines share computing time.\n\nThe ARPA project matured under Larry Roberts, and on September 2, 1969, the first node of the ARPANET came to life at UCLA: an Interface Message Processor, a minicomputer dedicated to the network's chores, linked to Leonard Kleinrock's Sigma 7 over a fifteen-foot cable. On the evening of October 29, 1969, a graduate student, Charley Kline, tried to log in from UCLA to a machine at Stanford Research Institute, some 350 miles away. He typed L, confirmed it had arrived over a telephone handset, typed O, confirmed again, typed G - and the machine crashed. The first words ever sent across the new network were the two letters LO, which worked out absurdly well: lo, and behold.\n\nFrom those two letters the network grew in ordered increments: four nodes by December 1969, electronic mail crossing the ARPANET by 1971, and in 1973-74 Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn's design for Transmission Control Protocol and Internet Protocol, published in 1974 as \"A Protocol for Packet Network Intercommunication,\" which let heterogeneous networks interconnect - the internet as a network of networks. On January 1, 1983 the ARPANET converted wholesale to TCP/IP, and in 1989 Tim Berners-Lee, working at CERN, added the layer nobody had thought to commodity: the World Wide Web, with hypertext addresses to click. Kleinrock's \"lo\" was followed, in about fifty years, by a system carrying most of humanity's chattering and commerce.",
      "mechanism": "The internet works by packet switching, which is best appreciated by contrast with the telephone system. A phone call reserves a dedicated circuit from one end to the other; if the middle fails, the call dies. The internet instead chops every communication - an email, a web page, a video stream - into small packets, each carrying the sender's address, the destination, and a sequence number. Each packet travels independently, bouncing through switching machines called routers that read the destination and forward the packet along the best available path, possibly different from its neighbors. Store-and-forward means each router holds a packet briefly and passes it on; the packets may arrive out of order, and the receiving machine reassembles them by sequence number.\n\nThis design is why the network can be killed almost nowhere and everywhere. There is no single switch whose loss downgrades the world; if one router dies, the others adapt their forwarding tables, and packets simply take another road. It also makes the network indifferent - it does not care what the packets contain, so the same plumbing carries mail, files, video, and code, which is why engineers say it is a dumb network with smart ends: all the intelligence for reassembly and error correction lives in the computers at the edge.\n\nLayering is the second pillar of the mechanism. TCP and IP divide the work: IP handles moving packets from machine to machine by address - the postal service - while TCP handles the reliable delivery, retransmitting what was lost and reassembling the message - the registered letter with a signature. Above those two sit the applications with their own languages of etiquette, from email to the web's HTTP. Each layer trusts the one below and ignores the details of the ones above, which is how fifty years of new applications have kept running on the same unchanged postal system.",
      "significance": "The internet's significance is that it removed geography from most forms of communication, and nobody has yet found a way to put it back. It collapsed the cost of reaching another human or machine to near zero, which reordered media, business, publishing, friendship, protest, work, and war. The design decision that began as Cold War paranoia - survivable, decentralized, best effort - turned out to be precisely the architecture that could scale to five billion users, because a network with no center tolerates growth by simply adding more of itself.\n\nIts later evolution brought the compromises of its innocence: a network built open by design had to be armored afterward with cryptography (see cryptography), because the same indifferent packets that carry your love letters carry your bank card numbers, and the same openness that empowered assembly can confuse authority. The internet also settled a couple of philosophical disputes quietly. Computers, it showed, are not islands; a machine alone is a calculator, but a machine on the network is a citizen. And the ARPANET's design discipline - no single point of failure - proved itself in a form neither Baran nor Davies quite promised: as a commons that nearly everyone now assumes, like air, and misses only when it breaks.",
