      "story": "Artificial intelligence began in the summer of 1956 with a workshop, a modest proposal, and enormous overconfidence. The proposal, written by John McCarthy with Marvin Minsky, Nathaniel Rochester, and Claude Shannon, was titled \"A Proposal for the Dartmouth Summer Research Project on Artificial Intelligence,\" and it is worth quoting because it is the field's founding document and its founding boast: \"The study is to proceed on the basis of the conjecture that every aspect of learning or any other feature of intelligence can in principle be so precisely described that a machine can be made to simulate it.\" The workshop assembled the discipline's first community, gave it its name, and set about proving the conjecture. The evidence came slowly and the surprises arrived on schedule.\n\nThe 1950s and 1960s produced genuine spectacles: Allen Newell and Herbert Simon's Logic Theorist proving theorems; Joseph Weizenbaum's ELIZA in 1966 passing for a therapist to people who knew better and to some who did not; Terry Winograd's SHRDLU moving blocks in a toy world in 1972. Each triumph moved the goalposts, which is the discipline's characteristic curse: the moment a machine learns to do something, the something is reclassified as not really intelligence, leaving intelligence perpetually a step ahead of the machines. The Dartmouth optimism collided with the computational realities of the 1970s, funding stalled - the Lighthill report in Britain in 1973 did its share - and domestic AI entered what the faithful called a winter.\n\nThe field has since cycled through eras with a peculiar rhythm: expert systems in the 1980s that encoded specialist knowledge into rules and excited industry; then another winter; then a statistical awakening in which machines stopped being told the rules and started learning them from data. Neural networks, long mocked as biologically flavored curve-fitting, revived once there were chips fast enough and corpora large enough to train them. Deep learning crossed public consciousness in 2012 with AlexNet's image-recognition triumph, then AlphaGo beat the world champion at Go in 2016, and by the 2020s large language models - transformer networks trained on enormous text - could generate essays, code, and conversation fluent enough to be mistaken for a person in one context and angrily for a machine in another. Sixty-five years after Dartmouth, the conjecture has not been settled; what has been settled is that intelligence is harder to fake than the founders hoped, and easier to underrate than the skeptics feared.",
      "mechanism": "The mechanisms of artificial intelligence have changed with its eras, and each is a different theory of what thinking is. The early era ran on search: treat a problem as a space of possible states and look through that space for a path to the goal. Chess programs grew strong by evaluating millions of positions with pruning heuristics, and the Logic Theorist's theorem-proving was the same idea with reasoning steps. The expert-system era stored expertise as if-then rules - if the patient has a fever and a rash, consider measles - and the machine's \"intelligence\" was the quality of the rules a human specialist had encoded.\n\nThe modern era is statistical. A neural network is a layered collection of simple computing units, each computing a weighted sum of its inputs and passing it through a nonlinearity; training adjusts the weights - millions or trillions of them - so that the network's outputs increasingly match its training targets. Deep learning means many layers, each able to detect progressively more abstract features: edges, then shapes, then faces, then concepts. The transformer, the architecture behind large language models, adds an attention mechanism: when processing a word, the network weighs how much every other word in the sentence should influence it, letting it track dependencies across long distances - the pronoun agreeing with its noun a paragraph earlier, the topic threading through a whole essay.\n\nWhat all these mechanisms share is that they work by approximation, not by simulation of a mind. A language model does not understand the sentence in the way a person does; it has learned the statistical shape of language and can reshuffle its learned patterns into plausible, even brilliant, continuations. The philosophical scandal of modern AI is precisely that learning patterns proves so useful in practice - that a network with no claimed understanding can still translate, classify, summarize, and compose. Whether that counts as the thinking Turing asked about in 1950 is the question the field keeps refusing to retire.",
      "significance": "Artificial intelligence matters because it relocated intelligence from a presumed property of the mind to a movable engineering quantity. The Dartmouth conjecture - that intelligent behavior can be simulated by a machine - has driven not only software but the culture around it: the thought experiment of Turing's imitation game, the scare-magazine \"electronic brain\" rhetoric, the chess showdowns, the government commissions, the science fiction, and the policy debates have all treated machine intelligence as a problem to be managed rather than an impossibility to be dismissed. Every one of those framings was an argument about what is special about people, conducted in public.\n\nIts practical significance is now diffuse: recommendation systems that sort your attention, vision systems that read your number plates, speech systems that answer your calls, and generative systems that draft your prose are all products of the same bet, and institutions have staked their fortunes on it. The discipline also holds a mirror to its creators, for the definition of intelligence keeps receding from whatever machines just learned to do - the so-called AI effect - and the most honest verdict on the state of the art may be the field's own grumble that if it works, they stop calling it AI.",
