      "summary": "The mathematician who made the programmable machine thinkable, and then cracked the unbreakable. In 1936 Turing defined the logical machine that bears his name — an abstract device able to compute anything computable — and with it proved that some problems simply cannot be solved. That stirrings-in-Bletchley work, on a problem of pure logic, became the blueprint of every computer.\n\nAt Bletchley Park during the war he led the effort to break the German Enigma cipher, shortening the war by an estimated two years. Afterward he designed the automatic computing engine, asked \"Can machines think?\" in a 1950 paper that founded the Turing test and modern AI, and was prosecuted for homosexuality in 1952 — chemically castrated rather than imprisoned. He died in 1954, aged 41, of cyanide poisoning, ruled suicide. In 2009 the British government apologised; in 2013 he received a royal pardon.\n",
