      "summary": "The rear-admiral who taught machines to speak English. Grace Hopper joined the Navy as an officer in World War II and was assigned to the Harvard Mark I, the first large-scale American computer. Her refusal to accept that programming meant ones and zeros led her to build compilers — programs that translate human-friendly code into machine language — and then in 1959 to COBOL, the business language that still runs the world's banks.\n\nShe was a teacher, a lifelong sailor, and a legend of the field: the word \"bug\" was hauled out of a real moth she removed from a relay at Harvard. She retired from the Navy as its oldest serving officer, and in 2016 was posthumously awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom.\n",
