      "clipping": {
        "newspaper": "The Philadelphia Inquirer",
        "date": "1952",
        "headline": "A PROGRAMMER TEACHES THE MACHINE TO READ WORDS",
        "body": "A United States Navy officer has demonstrated a compiler that translates ordinary-looking statements into machine code, a feat previously considered the machine’s own. Her colleagues told her it could not be done; the computer accepted it with little complaint."
      },
      "story": "Grace Brewster Murray was born in New York City on 9 December 1906 into a family that insisted she be educated like her brother. She took a degree in mathematics and physics at Vassar in 1928, then a master's at Yale in 1930, and in 1934 became the first woman to receive a PhD in mathematics from Yale. She taught at Vassar, published little, and might have lived a quiet academic life. The war changed that: in December 1943 she joined the United States Naval Reserve as a lieutenant, and was assigned to Howard Aiken's computation project at Harvard — the Mark I, a room-sized electromechanical calculator.\n\nAt Harvard she discovered that programming the machine meant rewiring patch panels and scheduling its innards. She wrote the Mark I's operation manual, and her team's logbook for 9 September 1947 records the machine finding its first insect: \"First actual case of bug being found.\" They taped the moth to the page. She learned that human beings, not machines, were the unreliable element, and set herself against the engineering contempt for users that defined her field.\n\nThe Navy released her after the war, and then declined to take her back: she was too old and too needed at Vassar. So she went where the future was, joining the Eckert-Mauchly Computer Corporation in 1949 — the firm building UNIVAC, America's first commercial computer. In 1952 she demonstrated the A-0 compiler, a program that would translate symbolic mathematical statements into machine code. Her superiors doubted it could be done; she answered that computers only understood arithmetic, and that hers was a business machine that had never been asked to compile before. For years nobody would use it, and for years she kept the compiler a secret weapon, one of the founding acts of her neighbour the-first-compiler and of all our programming languages.\n\nShe spent 1959 chairing the committee that wrote COBOL, insisting the language read like English so that managers could audit the ledgers their machines processed — a decision that is why billions of bank transactions still run on COBOL today. Recalled to active duty in 1967 as a retired commander, she became the Navy's authority on standardising programming languages, rose to captain in 1973, commodore in 1983, and rear admiral in 1985 — retiring in 1986 at seventy-nine as its oldest serving officer. She died on 1 January 1992 and was buried at Arlington National Cemetery; in 2016 she received the Presidential Medal of Freedom. She was also a celebrity of science, famous for passing out a piece of wire about a foot long — eleven and three-quarter inches, she would explain, is exactly how far light travels in a nanosecond — the length, she said, of a unit she understood completely.",
      "mechanism": "A compiler is a translator, and Hopper built the first ones that were not merely assemblers. A source program written in a human-friendly language — her A-0 took symbolic mathematical statements — is first parsed into tokens, checked for grammar, and resolved against the machine's behaviour; the compiler then emits code in the machine's own language, plus the tables and hooks by which the running program calls the operating system. Her deeper argument, revolutionary in 1952, was that the computer itself could do this translation: the machine, programmed once, could turn its owner's English into its own bytes, and could be given new words as new data. This is why compilers and the programming-languages they interpret are separable from any particular hardware.\n\nIt looks obvious now, and it was resisted savagely then. Her technique was to adopt the standards her critics insisted were impossible, and to let the machine prove them wrong. COBOL made the mechanism political: verbs like ADD and MOVE, a DATA DIVISION readable by auditors, and a common syntax across vendors so that programs, unlike people, did not stop being useful when they changed employers. The compiler is also the reason her name is welded to programming itself — the obsessive loop of edit, compile, run, debug is the direct descendant of the A-0, and the debugging whose very vocabulary she helped fix was born in a moth, a logbook, and a Harvard relay.",
      "significance": "Hopper's significance is that she made programming an act of writing rather than of soldering. By compiling human-readable language into machine code she moved software from the electrician's bench to the desk, and from the few to the many — the precondition for programming-languages, the personal computer, and ultimately the discipline of human-computer-interaction, which rests on the same conviction that machines should meet people where people speak. COBOL's longevity is her quiet monument: built in 1959, still running the world's financial plumbing.\n\nShe was also the field's first great teacher and its most quotable conscience. Her insistence on training young officers, her style of asking forgiveness rather than permission, and her war against \"we have always done it this way\" all became institutional doctrine. As a woman who reached rear admiral and a doctorate in a field that repeatedly tried to exclude her, she was both exception and lesson: the computer industry that kept women out of its history borrowed heavily from the woman who made its languages.",
