      "clipping": {
        "newspaper": "The Illustrated London News",
        "date": "1843",
        "headline": "THE COUNTESS WHO SAW AHEAD OF THE ENGINE",
        "body": "Augusta Ada, Countess of Lovelace, has published the most complete account yet of Charles Babbage’s analytical engine, and in her notes imagines the machine doing more than arithmetic: manipulating symbols, composing music, ‘weaving algebraic patterns.’ The engine itself remains unbuilt."
      },
      "story": "Augusta Ada Byron was born in London on 10 December 1815, the only legitimate child of George Gordon, Lord Byron, and his able, intensely respectable wife, Anne Isabella Milbanke. The marriage collapsed within weeks of her birth and Byron fled England for good; Ada never knew him. Her mother, terrified that her daughter would inherit the poet's dangerous imagination, decided education was the vaccine and had the girl taught mathematics seriously from a young age, by tutors including William Frend and, later, by correspondence, Augustus De Morgan of University College London. The strategy misfired pleasantly: Ada was mathematically gifted, and the imagination her mother feared she would keep using in poetry she spent on systems.\n\nAt seventeen, at a London soirée hosted by the mathematician Mary Somerville, she was introduced to Charles Babbage and saw his Difference Engine — a clockwork of cogs that tabulated polynomials. She was too young to notice a short walk away that he was switching its gears mentally to a grander \"Analytical Engine\": an unbuilt machine of mill and store, controlled not by gears but by Jacquard punch cards, which could, Babbage insisted, be told to do any arithmetic. Ada married William King in 1835 and in 1838 became Countess of Lovelace. She kept up with Babbage, translating, annotating, and — unlike almost everyone — understanding.\n\nIn 1842 an Italian engineer, Luigi Menabrea, published a clear account of the Analytical Engine. Ada translated it into English and, at Babbage's urging, added her own notes. They ran to three times the length of the memoir. In Note A she gave the phrase the world remembers: the Engine \"weaves algebraic patterns, just as the Jacquard loom weaves flowers and leaves.\" In Note G she wrote out a table of operations for computing Bernoulli numbers — a looping procedure, complete with conditional branching, that is conventionally honoured as the first computer program, though it ran only on paper, for the machine was never built. And in the same Note G she planted the objection that all later artificial intelligence would have to answer: \"The Analytical Engine has no pretensions whatever to originate anything. It can do whatever we know how to order it to perform.\"\n\nShe died on 27 November 1852, at thirty-six, worn down by illness — probably cancer — and by the chronic pain that had punctuated her whole life. Her short, strange arc made her, in the history books her neighbours algorithms and programming-languages keep, the prophet of software in a hardware century: the first person to see that a machine taught arithmetic could be taught anything expressible as symbols. Alan Turing, a hundred years later, would open his own founding paper by naming her two objections and answering them.",
      "mechanism": "The Analytical Engine, built nowhere, worked as follows: a store held numbers, a mill performed arithmetic on them, and Jacquard-style punched cards fed it instructions, including conditional jumps and loops. Babbage's genius was the mechanism; Lovelace's was to insist the mechanism was not the point. In her reading, the engine separated the process of computing from any particular computation — the operation from the data — which is exactly the split the stored-program computer (see the-stored-program-computer) would one day make physical. She called this \"the science of operations\", and argued that its subject matter was symbolic, not numerical: anything that could be expressed in a language of symbols could, in principle, be operated on.\n\nHer Bernoulli program works on that premise. The table in Note G steps through successive Bernoulli numbers by initialising values in the store, cycling a loop under a controlling variable, and branching conditionally when the cycle completes — the same control flow taught in any first programming course. Read strictly, it confirms Babbage's claim about arithmetic and nothing more. Read generously, it is the first documented algorithm written to be executed, which is how her neighbours in algorithms like to read it. The engine never ran, so the program's destiny was to be read, argued over, and finally recognised as the opening proof that software is a real thing the moment someone can imagine it.",
      "significance": "Lovelace's significance is one of vision rather than hardware. In a decade when a calculating machine was regarded as a superior abacus, she asserted that computing could manipulate music, logic, symbols, and eventually the texture of any formal system — anticipating programming-languages, and indeed the personal computer, by a century. Her phrase \"weaves algebraic patterns\" gave computing its first metaphor of software as something made, not merely executed.\n\nShe also established, in one note, the essential critique of machine intelligence: that a machine can only do what it has been ordered to do. Turing took her so seriously that he labelled it \"Lady Lovelace's objection\" in Computing Machinery and Intelligence — the counter-argument his imitation game was designed to dispatch. That her legacy should be both a founding program and a founding objection is fitting: she stands at the start of the conversation software still has with itself. The United States Department of Defense named its 1980s programming language, Ada, after her; the \"Ada Lovelace Day\" honours women in science; and her century-old conviction that imagination is a scientific faculty remains the working philosophy of everyone who ever sketched an interface before building it.",
