      "summary": "The polymath who put the stored program into the computer. John von Neumann, born in Budapest, moved through mathematics, quantum physics and game theory, and in 1945 wrote the report that named the \"stored-program\" architecture — instructions held in the same memory as data — now called the von Neumann architecture and used by essentially every general-purpose computer ever built.\n\nHe consulted on the ENIAC, designed the EDVAC, helped found game theory with Oskar Morgenstern in 1944, and brought his prodigious mind to the Manhattan Project and the mathematics of nuclear weapons and the Cold War. He died of cancer in 1957, aged 53, at Walter Reed Hospital working on the hydrogen bomb.\n",
