      "community": "computation",
      "story": "Long before the first computer was built, the rumor of a thinking machine was abroad, and the press was its enthusiastic landlord. When ENIAC was finished in 1945, the newspapers could not resist calling it an electronic brain; when it hummed through problems that took clerks a month, the headlines grew shriller. The public was learning, as its descendants still are, to be simultaneously awed and terrified. The dread was not entirely unearned: a machine that could hold a conversation in numbers, decide a firing problem in seconds, and keep its poise when its human partner slept was, on paper, the sort of thing that proposes you sell it your house. The anxiety and the reverence rose and fell in alternating decades, and Turing timed his intervention almost exactly.\n\nIn 1950 Alan Turing published \"Computing Machinery and Intelligence,\" which asked its question straight: \"I propose to consider the question, 'Can machines think?'\" He replaced that question with a test he called the imitation game - an interrogator, a hidden human, a hidden machine, and the practical judgment of whether the interrogator can tell them apart - and he predicted, with fifty years' lead, that machines would win it convincingly enough to satisfy most questioners. The paper made the thinking machine respectable, quotable, and contested all at once, and the newspapers adopted the term \"thinking machine\" as their headline of choice. Turing's confidence set the tone of the discussion for decades.\n\nThe machine after Turing kept the story alive by making the impossible routine. In 1968 Stanley Kubrick's HAL 9000 dramatized the terror with exquisite politeness; in 1997 Deep Blue beat the world chess champion; in 2016 AlphaGo won at Go, the game computer scientists had long used as proof that machines could not calculate their way to mastery. Each landmark did the same rhetorical work: it moved the boundary. Whatever the machine had just done was, by the time the headline was set, reclassified as not really thinking - which is the curious dance the public and the field perform together. The thinking machine has always been precisely as intelligent as it has failed to be last year, which is why the phrase remains, after seventy years, both a cliche and a live question.",
      "mechanism": "The imitation game, Turing's proposed test, is the mechanism that turned a metaphysical question into an experiment. Three parties sit in separate rooms: an interrogator, a human respondent, and a machine. The interrogator types questions and receives typed answers from both hidden parties, trying to decide which is the human. The machine wins if it is mistaken for the human often enough. Turing's point was that thinking, if it is to be discussed at all, may as well be discussed behaviourally: if a machine can perform as a person in such a conversation, then whatever is being tested is at least being exercised.\n\nThere is a second mechanism at work in the public story, harder to draw and easy to demonstrate: the shifting goalposts. In the 1940s computing machines were called brains for doing arithmetic; by the 2020s a machine that writes an essay about consciousness is commonly told that it cannot possibly be thinking because it only predicts text. The tests by which the public awards or withholds the label intelligence keep retreating ahead of the machinery - the so-called AI effect. Watch the sequence: the calculator that was a wonder becomes a tool; the chess player that was a marvel becomes a toy; each new competence is absorbed into the category of mere computation.\n\nWhich is why the phrase \"thinking machine\" is best read as a social instrument rather than a taxonomy. It lets a society negotiate, in public, what it is prepared to call intelligence and what it reserves for itself. The mechanism of the conversation is constant: name the machine's achievement, decide whether it counts, and then find the next thing that does not. The machine's architecture changes; the debate's architecture does not.",
      "significance": "The thinking machine is the story the public told itself about computing while the engineers were busy, and that story shaped the technology as much as the transistors did. It set expectations high enough to fund the field and low enough to disappoint it - the alternating headlines of electronic-brained genius and glorified calculator that have accompanied every milestone from ENIAC to the language models. It also licensed a very twentieth-century rearrangement: thinking, which had been presumed the private property of minds, became a property that could be attributed to, withheld from, and argued about in respect of a machine.\n\nIts lasting significance is philosophical and political at once. Turing's question forced a public reckoning with what counts as a mind, and that reckoning has real consequences, from job displacement to robot rights debates to the heavy legal and military responsibilities placed on automatic systems that \"decide.\" The fear has always been a little over-painted - the machine's greatest talent remains making plausible sentences - and the awe a little under-painted - the sentence-making genuinely restructures the information economy. In between those two mistakes, the thinking machine has done its real work: it has made the species ask, in public and with mirrors at hand, what it means when a machine can do what only minds used to do.",
