      "story": "Curves had been tempting mathematicians for two thousand years before anyone could handle them. Archimedes found the area of a circle and the volume of a sphere by exhaustion, a limit in all but name. Fermat found tangents to curves by a near-miss of the same idea. The seventeenth century's indivisibles, Cavalieri's and Torricelli's, sliced areas into infinitely thin strips. The calculus was not conjured from nothing; it was the discovery that all these scattered tricks were one subject.\n\nTwo men found it at once, from different directions, in different notation. Isaac Newton, sent down from Cambridge in 1665 by the plague, invented his method of fluxions in the quiet of Woolsthorpe: quantities flow, and their velocities are fluxions. His mathematics was written for physics and delivered, in the Principia of 1687, with mechanics attached. Gottfried Leibniz, a diplomat and polymath in Paris from 1672, built the differential calculus instead: differences dy and dx, the integral sign as a stretched s, and in 1684 the first published paper on the subject, the Nova Methodus. The two knew of each other's work obliquely; each thought himself first.\n\nThe priority dispute did real damage. From 1699 the two camps bickered, and in 1712 the Royal Society, with Newton himself reviewing the evidence, declared for Newton. European mathematics split, and British mathematics, loyal to fluxions and Newton's dot, lagged for a century behind the Leibnizian mainland with its clearer notation. History now credits both men with independent discovery, and the feud reads as the cautionary tale of two great minds and one small vanity.\n\nThere remained the scandal of foundations. What, exactly, was an infinitesimal? Berkeley's Analyst of 1734 gave the answer that still stings: ghosts of departed quantities. The eighteenth century used the calculus magnificently while unable to say what it was. D'Alembert pointed to limits; Cauchy, in the Cours d'analyse of 1821, wrote the limit down rigorously; Weierstrass, in Berlin lectures of the 1850s and 60s, distilled it into the epsilon-delta form. The calculus became analysis, and the scandal became a science.\n\nToday the calculus is the working language of continuous change. It writes Newton's laws, Maxwell's equations, Einstein's field equations; it drives engineering optimisation and economics' marginal analysis; it is the spine of every differential equation, which is to say of almost every quantitative science. Its name, calculus, the Latin for counting pebbles, is an irony that would have amused its founders: the mathematics of the infinite began as a pebble, and became the grammar of motion.",
      "mechanism": "The calculus is two operations with one underpinning. Differentiation measures the rate of change: the slope of the tangent, obtained as the limit of secant slopes as the interval shrinks to zero. If position is a function of time, the derivative is velocity. A worked example: for f(x) = x-squared, the derivative is 2x, found by taking the secant slope 2x + h and letting h vanish. This is The Derivative in one line.\n\nIntegration measures accumulation: the area under a curve. Slice the region into vertical strips, approximate each by a thin rectangle, and let the strips grow infinitely many and infinitely thin; the total area is the limit of the sums. Leibniz's integral sign, an elongated s for sum, records exactly this: a sum with infinitely many terms. If velocity is a function of time, the integral over an interval is the total distance travelled.\n\nThe Fundamental Theorem of Calculus binds the two. The derivative of the accumulated area function is the original curve: differentiating the integral gives back what you started with, and integrating a derivative recovers the change. Slope and area, rate and total, are inverse operations. This single discovery is why the calculus is one subject rather than two, and why the two halves of the subject always seemed to know about each other before anyone could say how.\n\nBoth operations rest on the limit, and the limit is what makes the whole thing rigorous. Newton and Leibniz had worked with infinitesimals, quantities smaller than any assignable size, which Berkeley mocked as ghosts of departed quantities. Cauchy and Weierstrass replaced the ghosts with the epsilon-delta condition: every instantaneous rate and every infinite sum is the limit of finite, checkable quantities. The machinery below The Derivative is the same machinery that defines the integral, and Limits is its load-bearing floor.",
      "significance": "The calculus is the mathematics of change, and the world is mostly change. It writes the laws of physics, Newton's mechanics, Maxwell's electromagnetism, Einstein's general relativity, the equations of quantum mechanics, because those laws say how things change, and how things change is the derivative. It computes the areas and sums, the integrals, that turn those laws into predictions: orbits, fields, populations, prices. Any science that studies continuous processes is the calculus in a different uniform.\n\nInside mathematics, the calculus reshaped the discipline. From it grew analysis, differential equations, and the theory of functions; its rigorisation through limits gave modern mathematics its standard of proof. The concept of the Function became the calculus's main object, and the machinery of Limits became the foundation under everything. The calculus also exported its methods: economics adopted it for marginal analysis, engineering for optimisation and control, and statistics for the distribution of errors.\n\nIts story is the archetype of mathematics correcting itself. Two rival founders, a priority dispute that split Europe, a foundation so shaky that a bishop could mock it, and finally a rigorous rebuilding, the arc from genius through scandal to science is the subject's own autobiography. That a method born to measure tangents and areas should become the shared language of physics, engineering, and economics is the argument, if any were needed, that the mathematics of change is the mathematics of everything that matters.",
