      "story": "The mathematics of the uncertain was born in a Paris gambling salon. In 1654 the Chevalier de Mere, a nobleman who took his dice seriously, pressed Blaise Pascal with two puzzles: how to split the stakes when a game is interrupted, and whether betting on at least one six in four rolls is fair. Pascal wrote to Pierre de Fermat in Toulouse, and the letters between them in that summer of 1654 are, by general agreement, the founding document of probability theory. The two men disagreed at first, Pascal counted while Fermat listed, then converged on the same answers, and in the machinery they built can be found combinations, expectation, and the modern habit of treating chance as mathematics rather than mood.\n\nThe idea spread slowly, exactly as the node's summary grumbles. Christiaan Huygens, on hearing of the correspondence, wrote the first textbook in 1657, De Ratiociniis in Ludo Aleae, and introduced the expected value, the long-run average of a gamble. Jacob Bernoulli's Ars Conjectandi, published posthumously in 1713, proved the law of large numbers: repeated trials make the observed frequency settle down around the true probability. Thomas Bayes's essay on inverse probability appeared in 1763, and Laplace finally pulled the subject into a grand system in 1812 with his Theorie Analytique des Probabilites, the work that stated the version of the central limit theorem routinely used ever since. It genuinely took those two centuries to persuade anyone the subject was about real life rather than salon bets; Laplace and his contemporaries wore it on their sleeves in astronomy and the theory of errors, and by the 1900s it was the grammar of physics (see Quantum Mechanics) and of genetics.",
      "mechanism": "Probability asks: of all the ways things could go, how many are the ones I care about? For equally likely outcomes the rule is to count. Roll two fair dice. There are 6 x 6, that is 36, equally likely pairs. A total of seven comes from (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1), six pairs, so the probability of a seven is 6/36, one sixth, about 17%. The tiny claim is a counting fact, not a feeling.\n\nCounting alone needs help when outcomes are not equally likely, which is where expected value and the two great theorems come in. The law of large numbers says a coin's fraction of heads drifts toward 1/2 as you flip more: seven heads out of ten may happen, but seven hundred out of a thousand almost cannot. Conditional probability, what if we already know something, was the fruit of the interrupted-game puzzle, and Bayes's rule turns it around to answer the genuinely modern question: given what I have observed, how should I update my belief? That single reversal made probability a working theory of learning from evidence, and it is why a medical test's accuracy cannot be judged without also knowing how rare the disease is.",
      "significance": "Probability is the technology of doubt rightly spent. It underwrites insurance, the pricing of risk in finance, the design of clinical trials, weather prediction, and the entire language of modern machine learning: every email filter and recommendation engine is a Bayesian or statistical model wearing a disguise. It also gave physics its soft spot: in quantum mechanics the best predictions science has ever made are probability distributions, a fact Bohr and Einstein spent decades arguing about (see the Bohr-Einstein Debate).\n\nIts deepest legacy is philosophical. Probability forced the realisation that we do not live in a world of pure certainty, and that good decisions do not require it, only good arithmetic about what we do not know. Pascal himself, in the Pensees, used the fresh mathematics to price a wager about God, audacious if not decisive. Laplace phrased the whole programme with characteristic calm: the theory of probabilities is nothing but common sense reduced to calculus.",
