      "story": "Statistics grew from two different species of counting. The word itself comes from the Latin for state affairs: statistics began as the state's habit of counting people, plagues and prices. John Graunt, a London haberdasher, published Natural and Political Observations upon the Bills of Mortality in 1662, counting the weekly death bills and noticing, among other things, that more boys than girls were born; Edmund Halley, of comet fame, built the first serious life table in 1693 from the records of the city of Breslau, giving the insurance trade its actuarial machinery. Meanwhile probability was being invented in Paris (see Probability). The Belgian astronomer Adolphe Quetelet married the two strands in the 1830s: he measured, for men and then for whole nations, the famous bell-shaped distribution of errors, and declared the 'average man' an object of study.\n\nThe second century of statistics is a louder story. Francis Galton, Darwin's cousin, quantified heredity and in 1886 discovered what he called regression towards mediocrity: tall fathers' sons are, on average, less tall. The phenomenon, now called regression to the mean, is everywhere. Galton and Karl Pearson, the first holder of a dedicated statistics chair, built correlation and the chi-square test, and founded the journal Biometrika in 1901. William Gosset, brewing at Guinness, anonymously published the t-test for small samples in 1908 under the name 'Student'. Then came Ronald Fisher, working from 1919 at the Rothamsted Experimental Station: analysis of variance, the design of randomized experiments, the standard errors of everyday science, and in 1925 the book Statistical Methods for Research Workers that trained the century's researchers. In 1933 Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson formalised hypothesis testing, deciding, with stated odds, when a difference is too big to be coincidence.",
      "mechanism": "Statistics's working question is: is this real or is this noise? The trick is to characterise the noise first. Measure a class's heights, 170, 171, 172 centimetres, and the mean is 171. But the mean alone hides the spread, measured by the standard deviation. For the bell-shaped normal curve, which sums of independent small effects tend to approximate, the central limit theorem, about 68% of observations sit within one standard deviation of the mean, 95% within two, 99.7% within three. That yields the classic diagnostic: a measurement two standard deviations from the mean is surprising enough to notice.\n\nThe test of a hypothesis works by playing devil's advocate. Suppose a coin is claimed fair. Nine heads out of ten flips will happen about one time in a hundred if the coin really is fair. We compare our data against that null hypothesis: if what we saw is very unlikely under the null, we reject it. That probability, the famous p-value, is a measure of surprise, not a verdict of truth. Regression to the mean gives the matching caution: an extreme result is often followed by a more average one, which is why miracle cures that are not real nevertheless seem to work, patients who were at their worst tend to improve by chance alone, and only randomised, blinded trials can tell improvement from gravity.",
      "significance": "Statistics is the mathematics everyone actually uses. It decides whether a new vaccine works, whether a deck of cards is loaded, whether a poll's margin is five points or fifty, what a climate model's uncertainty is, and how much of a school's test-score rise is the school rather than the noise. It is the shared verdict machine of science, and the discipline that made the randomised controlled trial possible, first famously applied in the 1948 streptomycin trial against tuberculosis.\n\nIts power is also its burden. The same apparatus that proves a drug works can, mishandled, certify almost anything; the twentieth century's plainest lesson is that correlation is not causation and that p-values can be gamed. The presidential-grade joke about lies, damned lies and statistics, attributed by Mark Twain to Benjamin Disraeli, records exactly this tension: numbers are honest, but they are never free of the hands that wrote them down. Statistics is proof that mathematics is at its best when it is humbled by data rather than mistrustful of it.",
