      "gift": "fl. 300 BC",
      "summary": "The geometer whose textbook was the world's standard for over two thousand years. Euclid of Alexandria composed the Elements around 300 BC: thirteen books that build all of geometry from five postulates and a handful of common notions, proving every theorem in order — the model of what \"rigour\" means in mathematics.\n\nThe Elements taught mathematics its method: start with axioms you cannot prove, then prove everything else. Euclid also wrote on number theory, and the \"algorithm\" for finding the greatest common divisor still bears his name. For centuries, to be educated was to have read Euclid; Abraham Lincoln kept a copy in his saddlebags.\n",
