      "story": "About Euclid the man almost nothing is known, and it mattered not at all. No biography survives, no portrait, not even a firm birthdate. Everything we think we know comes down from Proclus, a Neoplatonic philosopher writing in Athens some seven centuries after the fact: Euclid lived in Alexandria in the time of Ptolemy I, the Macedonian general who ruled Egypt and turned his new capital into a city of books. He 'put together the Elements,' Proclus reported, arranging the theorems of Eudoxus, perfecting the work of Theaetetus, and giving rigorous demonstration to what his predecessors had only loosely established. Proclus also handed down the one anecdote that stuck: Ptolemy, a busy king, asked whether there was a shorter road to geometry than the Elements, and Euclid replied that there was no royal road to geometry. The story is probably the same one told of Menaechmus and Alexander the Great; it does not matter. It is exactly what the book itself would have wanted us to believe of its author.\n\nThe Elements, composed around 300 BC, is thirteen books of argument. It opens with definitions, five postulates â€” of which the parallel postulate is the most famous and the most fragile â€” and five common notions that hold in all reasoning. From those slender premises Euclid proves 465 propositions in an unbroken chain: triangles, parallels, circles, ratios, and finally the theory of the regular solids, crowned by the construction of the five Platonic solids and a proof that no other regular solid exists. A reader who accepts the premises is committed to every conclusion; that is the contract, and the point. The book was not original research so much as a masterpiece of organisation, the geometry of two centuries gathered, ordered and made irrefutable.\n\nThe method made Euclid the mathematics teacher of the ancient world and then of every world after. He is said to have founded a school at Alexandria, where students came to learn geometry by the long way; when one asked what he would gain from the study, Euclid is reported to have told his servant to give the young man threepence, since he must make a profit from what he learns. The remark, if it is his, is the wry humour of a man who knew exactly how unpleasant a shortcut through a proof usually is.\n\nFor two thousand years the Elements was mathematics. Copied by hand through the Middle Ages, translated into Arabic and Latin, printed in Venice in 1482 and reprinted in hundreds of editions, it was the standard textbook of geometry until the twentieth century. Abraham Lincoln studied it by candlelight in a log cabin to discipline his mind, and for centuries to be educated was to have read Euclid. The man is a silhouette; the author is the most-read mathematician who ever lived.",
      "mechanism": "The Elements is a machine for manufacturing certainty. Euclid lays down three kinds of starting material: definitions, which fix what the words mean; postulates, which state what may be done in geometry (that a straight line may be drawn from any point to any point, that a circle may be described with any centre and radius); and common notions, axioms true of everything, such as that if equals be added to equals, the wholes are equal. A proposition is then admitted only if it follows from these by accepted rules of argument. No theorem may borrow from a later one; each result is a brick set only on bricks already laid. This is the famous axiomatic method, and the Elements is its first full-scale working example.\n\nThe most consequential single choice is the fifth postulate, the parallel postulate: that if a straight line falls on two straight lines and makes the interior angles on one side less than two right angles, the two lines, produced indefinitely, meet on that side. It is clumsier than the other postulates and resisted proof for two thousand years, which turned out to be its gift: when Lobachevsky, Bolyai and Riemann finally replaced it with alternatives in the nineteenth century, they obtained coherent geometries that are not Euclidean â€” the curved geometries in which the physics of the modern world now lives.\n\nThe same machinery appears in Euclid's number theory, which he folded into Books VII to IX. Book VII sets out the algorithm for the greatest common divisor that still bears his name: subtract the smaller from the larger, repeat, and the number that finally divides evenly is the GCD â€” a step-by-step procedure, one of the oldest algorithms in the world, still taught in every introduction to computing. Book IX contains the proof that the primes are infinite: assume a complete list, multiply them all and add one, and the new number is divisible by none of them, so the list must have been incomplete. That argument, written in 300 BC, is still how we prove the result.",
      "significance": "Euclid's real achievement is the definition of proof itself. Before the Elements, mathematics was a loose collection of useful rules and plausible results; after it, mathematics was the discipline in which a conclusion could be forced by argument alone. Every later formal system â€” Hilbert's axiomatisation of geometry, set theory, the logic running behind computer programs â€” is Euclid's method in modern dress, and when twentieth-century logic asked what a proof really is, it answered in Euclid's terms: a chain of statements from accepted axioms. The geometry node on this map is Euclid's geometry, and the proof node is his method.\n\nThe parallel postulate turned out to be the hinge of the modern world. Two millennia of failed attempts to prove it produced, in the end, non-Euclidean geometry â€” and that geometry is the mathematics of general relativity, the frame in which gravity is the curvature of space and time. The man who formalised geometry quietly supplied the frame Einstein would bend. Meanwhile the algorithm in Book VII is a founding ancestor of the step-by-step procedure that al-Khwarizmi's books would name for a different man and that now runs inside every computer.\n\nFor two thousand years the Elements was the most widely read mathematics book on Earth, and its author the model of the mathematician. 'There is no royal road to geometry,' the saying goes; the book made sure of that. But it built the road that everyone since has walked.",
