      "summary": "The mathematician whose name became the word algorithm and whose book named algebra. Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi worked at the House of Wisdom in Baghdad in the ninth century. His Kitab al-jabr wal-muqabala — \"the compendious book on calculation by completion and balancing\" — set down a systematic method for solving linear and quadratic equations, and gave the field its name: algebra.\n\nHis younger treatise on calculation with the Indian numerals, translated into Latin as Algoritmi de numero Indorum, gave the West place-value arithmetic and bequeathed his own name to the procedure: algorithm. From Baghdad to the world, the two streams — method and number — run from this one man.\n",
