      "summary": "The philosopher who, lost in the calculus race, also invented the language of logic. Leibniz developed the infinitesimal calculus independently of Newton in the 1670s and beat him into print with his symbols — the d of differentials and the ∫ of integrals are his. The priority dispute between London and Hanover poisoned European mathematics for a generation.\n\nLeibniz was also a diplomat, a librarian, and the dreamer of a universal symbolic language — a calculus of reasoning in which arguments would be settled like sums. That dream, written into his 1666 dissertation on combinations, became one engine of formal logic, and he glimpsed a binary arithmetic of ones and zeros that three centuries later would be the language of every computer.\n",
