      "summary": "A mathematician from West Virginia who handed economics its deepest simple idea. Nash, at twenty-one and still a graduate student at Princeton, proved that every finite game of strategy has at least one equilibrium point — a set of choices from which no player benefits by moving alone. The result published in one page in 1950, earned him the Nobel Prize in 1994.\n\nHis later life was shadowed by schizophrenia, and his recovery from decades of illness into a rebuilt scientific career made as deep an impression as the mathematics. He died in 2015 in a taxi crash in New Jersey, on the way home from a prize ceremony in Norway.",
