      "summary": "The mathematician whose numbers put Americans in orbit. Katherine Goble Johnson worked at NASA's Langley Research Center as one of the Black women of the \"computers\" pool, and her trajectory calculations were trusted for some of the most dangerous moments of the space race: John Glenn refused to fly until she had checked the computer's numbers by hand.\n\nShe calculated the trajectories for Alan Shepard's 1961 flight, Glenn's 1962 orbit, and the Apollo 11 and 13 missions in 1969 and 1970. In 2015 she received the Presidential Medal of Freedom, and the film Hidden Figures (2016) brought her story to the screen. She died in 2020 at 101.\n",
