      "summary": "The reluctant revolutionary who gave physics the quantum. In December 1900, trying to explain the colours of hot glowing bodies, Max Planck made an assumption he called \"an act of despair\": energy could only be emitted and absorbed in discrete packets, which he called quanta, of size proportional to frequency — the constant h now bears his name. He had not meant to overturn anything; he spent years trying to undo the idea.\n\nPlanck's quantum hypothesis, taken up by Einstein in 1905 and Bohr in 1913, became quantum mechanics. Planck himself, a conservative man of old Germany, watched his son executed by the Nazis and his institute bombed, and died in 1947 with a cigarette, asking several men with their own sacrifices to be seated beside him.\n",
