      "summary": "The Cavendish professor who proved the electron was a real particle and that atoms had insides. In 1897 J. J. Thomson bent cathode rays with electric and magnetic fields and measured the ratio of charge to mass of the ray's carriers — finding them much lighter than any known atom. He had discovered the electron, the first subatomic particle, and opened the atom to inspection.\n\nThomson proposed a plum-pudding model — electrons studded in a sphere of positive charge — which his own student Rutherford would demolish a decade later. He won the Nobel in 1906, taught a remarkable dynasty at the Cavendish, and died in 1940.\n",
