      "gift": "1845-1923",
      "summary": "The German physicist who, in November 1895, photographed the bones of his own hand and called the rays that did it \"X-strahlen\" — X, for unknown. Röntgen's X-rays passed through flesh, cast shadows on plates, and within a year surgeons were using them to find bullets and broken bones. It was the first new kind of radiation anyone had ever met.\n\nRöntgen took his Nobel prize in 1901, the very first ever awarded in physics. He declined to patent the discovery, gave the money to his university, and died in 1923, three months before he could watch X-rays shrink through a generation of use.\n",
