      "gift": "1852-1908",
      "summary": "The Frenchman who found radioactivity by leaving a photographic plate in a drawer. In 1896, testing whether minerals glowed after sunlight, Becquerel put uranium salts on a wrapped plate — and found it fogged with or without light. The uranium was radiating by itself, with no input of energy. He called it to the world, and Marie Curie named it radioactivity.\n\nBecquerel shared the 1903 Nobel in Physics with the Curies. The unit of radioactivity, the becquerel, carries his name. He died in 1908.\n",
