      "quotes": [
        {
          "quote": "What we observe is not nature itself but nature exposed to our method of questioning.",
          "said": "Werner Heisenberg"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Can nature possibly be so absurd as it seemed to us in these atomic experiments?",
          "said": "Werner Heisenberg",
          "context": "Recalling his night walks with Bohr, in Physics and Philosophy (1958)."
        },
        {
          "quote": "The first gulp from the glass of natural sciences will turn you into an atheist, but at the bottom of the glass God is waiting for you.",
          "said": "Werner Heisenberg",
          "context": "Attributed; reported conversation, widely quoted (e.g., in Capra, The Tao of Physics)."
        },
        {
          "quote": "I think that modern physics has definitely decided in favour of Plato. In fact the smallest units of matter are not physical objects in the ordinary sense; they are forms, ideas which can be expressed unambiguously only in mathematical language.",
          "said": "Werner Heisenberg",
          "context": "Physics and Philosophy (1958), summing up the Copenhagen reading of quantum reality."
        },
        {
          "quote": "Quantum theory provides us with a striking illustration of the fact that we can fully understand a connection though we can only speak of it in images and parables.",
          "said": "Werner Heisenberg",
          "context": "From his Gifford Lectures and related essays on the language of physics."
        }
      ],
      "story": "Werner Heisenberg was born in Würzburg in 1901 and grew up in Munich, a gifted, athletic student who taught himself calculus from a textbook at thirteen and read Plato in the original Greek. He studied physics under Arnold Sommerfeld at Munich and under Max Born at Göttingen, and in the autumn of 1922 spent the decisive early months at Bohr's institute in Copenhagen, where the twenty-one-year-old met the man he would revere for life. In 1925, recovering from hay fever on the rocky, windswept island of Helgoland, he became convinced that the pictures of electrons orbiting an atomic nucleus were meaningless, because no one could observe them. He rebuilt atomic physics as a mathematics of observable quantities - frequency, intensity, transition probabilities - in a scheme now called matrix mechanics. The result worked, but it was strange, and the mathematics was unfamiliar to almost everyone, including his own professor.\n\nIn 1927, while still at Bohr's institute in Copenhagen, Heisenberg found himself measuring position and momentum and discovered he could not have both exactly: 'the more precisely the position is determined, the less precisely the momentum is known in this instant, and vice versa.' This was no defect of apparatus but a law of nature - the uncertainty principle, which puts an absolute bound, governed by Planck's constant, on what any measurement can reveal. Attempts to see an electron with light would shake it; attempts to let it be, leave it unknowable. He was 25 and he had rewritten the meaning of measurement; he received the 1932 Nobel Prize in Physics (awarded 1933).\n\nHeisenberg stayed in Germany through the Nazi years. He led the German nuclear research program during the war, the 'Uranverein', which never produced a weapon - a fact that later raised the anguished question of whether German physicists obstructed their own work. In 1941 he visited Bohr in occupied Copenhagen in a stormy, much-debated meeting that ended the friendship. After the war he was interned with other German scientists at Farm Hall, near Cambridge; he rebuilt German physics as director of the Max Planck Institute for Physics, and worked for European research cooperation. In later years he tried to extend his ideas into a unified field theory and wrote about the philosophical implications of quantum mechanics, insisting that 'what we observe is not nature itself but nature exposed to our method of questioning.' He died at Munich in 1976. Bohr, with whom he had quarrelled, wrote to him of their old arguments as 'our never-to-be-forgotten discussions' - quantum mechanics' most human thread.",
      "mechanism": "Heisenberg's uncertainty principle states that the product of the uncertainties in position (dx) and momentum (dp) of a particle cannot be smaller than about h/4pi: dx times dp >= h/(4pi). The origin is physical: to locate a particle you must interact with it, and the very interaction that reveals position disturbs its momentum, with the disturbance set by Planck's constant. It is a principle about nature, not about the clumsiness of instruments - a truly measurement-less system has no definite simultaneous position and momentum. Matrix mechanics replaced the wave picture of Bohr's atom with tables of transition amplitudes, showing that quantum mechanics could be built entirely on observable quantities.",
      "significance": "Heisenberg gave quantum mechanics its deepest philosophical and mathematical forms: matrix mechanics (with Born and Jordan), the uncertainty principle, and - with Bohr - the Copenhagen interpretation that still dominates the field. The uncertainty principle became a founding idea of twentieth-century physics, echoed far beyond physics into the debates of philosophy. His wartime role and his celebrated post-war books also made him the most visible symbol of German science's moral reckoning.",
