      "gift": "1887-1961",
      "summary": "The Austrian who wrote the wave function and the dead cat. In 1926 Schrödinger replaced the jumping electrons of Bohr's atom with a wave equation that gives the whole mood of an electron at once — the ψ that carries quantum mechanics to this day. He shared the 1933 Nobel with Dirac.\n\nHis thought experiment of 1935, in which a cat in a box is neither alive nor dead until observed, became the world's most famous illustration of superposition — and was meant as a protest against an interpretation he hated. Schrödinger also wrote What is Life? (1944), which convinced a generation, including Watson, Crick and the future of molecular biology, that genes were encoded structure.\n",
