      "gift": "1902-1984",
      "summary": "The quiet Englishman who found, in the equations, an antimatter nobody asked for. In 1928 Dirac wrote a relativistic equation for the electron and found it came with a strange twin: for every state the equation permitted, it also permitted a mirror state with opposite charge. He spent his entire life refusing to oversell it, writing only that \"this would be a new kind of unanticipated particle, unknown to experimental physics\".\n\nWithin four years that particle was found: the positron, the first confirmed piece of antimatter. Dirac shared the 1933 Nobel with Schrödinger, and his equation became the foundation of particle physics. He was famously terse; a colleague once said of him that his words during a whole conference could be counted on the fingers of one hand.\n",
