      "gift": "1878-1968",
      "summary": "The physicist the Nobel forgot, and the one who understood fission before anyone announced it. Lise Meitner, an Austrian Jew working at the Kaiser Wilhelm Institute in Berlin, collaborated with chemist Otto Hahn on the puzzle of what happened when uranium absorbed neutrons. In 1938, after being forced to flee to Sweden, she received Hahn's letter describing \"barium\" in the residue — and with her nephew Otto Frisch, sitting on a log in the snow, computed that the nucleus had split: fission. It was she who first used the word.\n\nHahn alone received the 1944 Nobel in Chemistry. Meitner, the theorist, was nominated fourteen times and never won. She refused the invitation to join the Manhattan Project — she wanted no part of a bomb — and element 109, meitnerium, now carries her name.\n",
