      "clipping": {
        "newspaper": "Stockholm Dagblad",
        "date": "January 1939",
        "headline": "AN EXILE IN SWEDEN EXPLAINS THE SPLITTING OF THE ATOM",
        "body": "Fleeing the Reich the previous summer, the physicist Lise Meitner has spent her first months in exile re-reading her colleague Otto Hahn's puzzling lab notes, and has published the mathematics that makes sense of them: a nucleus split in two, releasing energy and more neutrons. The word ‘fission’ was suggested over the telephone by her nephew."
      },
      "story": "Lise Meitner was born in Vienna in 1878, the third of eight children of a Jewish lawyer. Girls were not admitted to the university matura, so she studied privately, passed the examinations, and in 1906 took her doctorate under Ludwig Boltzmann - the second woman to complete a doctorate in physics at the University of Vienna. In 1907 she moved to Berlin to learn radioactivity in the laboratory of Otto Hahn, but found the institute forbade women's entrance entirely. The director finally allowed her in through a side door to work in a converted woodworking shop, forbidden to use the laboratories and washrooms on the main floors. There the collaboration of Hahn and Meitner began, one of the great partnerships in the history of science, which lasted thirty years.\n\nIn 1912 Meitner joined the new Kaiser Wilhelm Institute for Chemistry; in 1917 she and Hahn discovered the element protactinium; in 1918 she became head of the institute's physics section; and in 1926 Berlin University made her its first woman professor - a consolation for her exclusion from chemistry's salaried posts. She helped build the picture of atomic nuclei through the 1920s and 1930s, but the Nazi rise brought ruin to her position: dismissed in 1933 from her university teaching, stripped in 1934 of the institute's women's committee chair. In July 1938, with her situation increasingly dangerous, she fled Germany via the Netherlands, helped by colleagues, and took refuge in Stockholm, at Manne Siegbahn's Nobel Institute for Physics - where, to her hurt, she was treated as a guest. Physics was, for a time, almost impossible.\n\nThat winter Hahn and Strassmann in Berlin bombarded uranium with neutrons and found barium in the products - a lighter element, which no one could believe. Hahn wrote to Meitner in December 1938, asking if she could suggest some fantastic explanation. She and her nephew Otto Frisch, working it out over the Christmas holidays at Kungälv, Sweden, in January 1939 understood it at once: the uranium nucleus had split roughly in half, releasing energy according to the mass defect, Einstein's E = mc squared. Frisch borrowed the word 'fission' from biology, and Meitner co-authored with him an explanation that Metner's Nature paper (11 February 1939) placed before the world. When the 1944 Nobel Prize went to Hahn alone, Meitner was excluded, an injustice that remains one of the Nobel's great scandals. Her later years brought honors: the Enrico Fermi Award, shared with Hahn and Strassmann in 1966, and, in 1997, the naming of element 109 as meitnerium. She died in Cambridge, England, in 1968. 'Life need not be easy,' she once wrote, 'provided only that it is not empty.'",
      "mechanism": "Meitner and Frisch explained fission as electrostatics and Einstein. A uranium nucleus (mass ~235) struck by a neutron splits into two roughly equal fragments; because the fragments' total mass is slightly less than the uranium's, the difference is released as energy, per E = mc^2 - about 200 MeV per fission, millions of times the chemical energy of a single molecule. They recognized that the binding energies known from mass measurements made such a split energetically possible, which was the crucial theoretical insight Hahn's chemistry could not supply.",
      "significance": "Meitner provided the physics of the most consequential discovery of the twentieth century - nuclear fission - and her name became a symbol of the unjust exclusions of science's gatekeepers. Her explanation made the atom bomb and nuclear power thinkable, and her life story helped expose the ways German science expelled and destroyed its Jewish members.",
