      "summary": "The radiochemist who split the nucleus and took the credit for the understanding he shared. Otto Hahn, at the Kaiser Wilhelm Institute in Berlin, spent the late 1930s bombarding uranium with neutrons and, in December 1938, found the element barium in the products — proof that the nucleus had broken apart. His long-time colleague Lise Meitner, exiled in Sweden, furnished the physics of the \"splitting\" and named it fission.\n\nHahn alone received the 1944 Nobel in Chemistry, a decision that remains one of the Nobel's most debated. Deeply scarred by the wartime use of his work, he campaigned after 1945 against nuclear weapons.\n",
