      "story": "Otto Hahn was born in Frankfurt am Main in 1879 and trained as an organic chemist, earning his doctorate at Marburg in 1901. Wishing for something more exotic than acetylenes, he accepted a position in 1904 at University College London under William Ramsay, where, studying the mineral barium, he found a new radioactive substance, radiothorium, and was converted at once from organic chemistry to radiochemistry - the fashionable and dangerous frontier science. In 1905-06 he worked at McGill University with Ernest Rutherford, absorbing Rutherford's dynamic new picture of radioactivity as atomic transmutation, and in 1907 he returned to Berlin, to the Kaiser Wilhelm Institute for Chemistry, where the young physicist Lise Meitner joined him. Their partnership produced discoveries for thirty years: radiothorium, mesothorium, radioactinium, and in 1917-18 protactinium, their names jointly on papers and jointly on the periodic table.\n\nAfter the First World War, Hahn built a respected school of radiochemistry, but his and Meitner's great opportunity came in late 1938, as physics fatally left him. Bombarding uranium with slow neutrons, Hahn and his assistant Fritz Strassmann expected the transuranium elements heavier than uranium, which physicists assumed were the products. Instead the chemical analysis kept turning up barium - element 56, mass roughly half of uranium's - an impossibility on the face of it. Hahn wrote to his exiled collaborator Meitner, who with her nephew Frisch showed him the physics of what had happened: the uranium nucleus, struck by a neutron, had split in half. The fission discovery was published in Naturwissenschaften in January 1939, and Meitner and Frisch supplied the interpretation in Nature in February. Hahn could not bring himself to name the process in the joint publication; the physics of fission, ironically, was never his. He received the 1944 Nobel Prize in Chemistry - Meitner did not - and was interned with fellow German scientists at Farm Hall in England after the war, where, hearing of Hiroshima, he is said to have been near despair, aware of what his discovery had enabled.\n\nAfter the war Hahn helped refound German science: acting president of the Kaiser Wilhelm Society (1946), then founding president of the Max Planck Society (1948-1960). He spent the rest of his life speaking, rather helplessly, against nuclear weapons and atomic war, insisting - with a certain official stubbornness that historians have debated - that he had hoped all along the German project would fail. He died in Göttingen in 1968.",
      "mechanism": "Naturally, fission had nothing to do with chemistry; the physics was worked out by Meitner and Frisch. Hahn and Strassmann's barium finding showed that neutron bombardment splits the uranium nucleus into two unequal parts with a large release of energy (the mass defect, via E = mc^2). The chemical proof - barium was identified among the products by careful fractional crystallization - was decisive because no known element was lighter than uranium yet chemically indistinguishable from barium. Fission was possible because uranium's nucleus is so large that electrostatic repulsion nearly equals the strong force binding it together; a slow neutron tip was enough.",
      "significance": "Hahn's chemistry made the discovery of fission possible; his name is on the 1944 Nobel Prize (Chemistry). The energy released in fission powers reactors and bombs, and the atom's history in consequence marks the twentieth century. Hahn's later role leading the Max Planck Society helped rebuild postwar German science, while his ambiguous wartime record and the Nobel committee's treatment of Meitner continue to generate debate.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "quote": "We keep coming to the terrible conclusion that our Ra isotopes behave not like Ra, but like Ba. ... Perhaps you can suggest some fantastic explanation.",
          "said": "Otto Hahn",
          "context": "Letter to Lise Meitner, 19 December 1938, on the discovery of fission."
        },
        {
          "quote": "I didn't think it would be possible for another twenty years.",
          "said": "Otto Hahn",
          "context": "At Farm Hall, England, reacting to the first radio reports of the Hiroshima bomb, 6 August 1945, as recorded in the Operation Epsilon transcripts."
        }
      ],
