      "id": "bertrand-competition",
      "type": "concept",
      "label": "Bertrand Competition",
      "tag": "Economics",
      "dates": [
        1883
      ],
      "location": "Paris",
      "summary": "The uncomfortable uncle of oligopoly theory. In an 1883 review of Cournot's book, the French mathematician Joseph Bertrand complained that if two firms with identical goods compete on price instead of quantity, the stable result is brutal: each undercuts the other until price equals marginal cost — the same outcome as perfect competition, with only two firms in the market.\n\nA whole school of economics grew out of that one complaint. The \"Bertrand paradox\" terrified oligopoly theorists, because it said competition on price with undifferentiated goods leaves no room for profit at all, and it pushed the profession toward product differentiation, capacity constraints and repeated games to explain why prices so rarely collapse in practice.",
