      "mechanism": "If both firms sell the same good, the one with the lower price takes the whole market. So the higher-priced seller shades its price to match, and the other shades again, in a race to the floor. The only resting point is where price equals marginal cost, where neither can profit by undercutting because neither can profit at all.\n",
      "significance": "The difference between Cournot and Bertrand is the difference between competing on how much you make and competing on what you charge. Real markets do both, and knowing which game the firms are playing — quantity or price — decides whether an industry makes comfortable profits or none.\n",
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Book review of Cournot’s Researches",
          "by": "Joseph Bertrand, Journal des Savants",
          "year": "1883"
        }
      ],
