      "mechanism": "A cartel must solve three problems: how to divide the total quota, how to detect cheating, and how to punish it. Game theory shows the cooperation it needs is only stable if the game is repeated — when today's cheating costs tomorrow's cooperation — or if the quota is enforced by an outside power with teeth.\n",
      "significance": "Cartels are where the clean mathematics of equilibrium meets the grubby business of enforcement. The word itself carries its history: a cartello was a written challenge between rivals, and when German industry borrowed it in the 1880s it kept the menace.\n",
