      "location": "Paris",
      "gift": "1822-1900",
      "summary": "A French mathematician remembered in economics for one grumpy review. In 1883 Bertrand reviewed Cournot's book in the Journal des Savants and objected that if two firms compete on price rather than quantity, the elegant duopoly equilibrium collapses: the rivals undercut each other until price hits marginal cost. The objection — a page or two long — founded an entire school.\n\nOutside economics, Bertrand was a serious analyst of probability and mathematical physics, a professor at the Collège de France, and a famously acid critic of loose reasoning. His name now stands in economics for exactly the kind of outcome he thought Cournot had missed.",
