      "gift": "1910-2004",
      "summary": "The Harvard economist who gave sticky prices their picture. Sweezy's 1939 paper \"Demand Under Conditions of Oligopoly\" produced the kinked demand curve — a diagram that explained, in one obvious picture, why prices in few-firm markets refuse to move even when costs change. It became a textbook fixture and survives there to this day.\n\nSweezy's politics were the far left; he co-founded Monthly Review in 1949 and remained its editor for half a century, building the most widely read Marxist journal in the United States. The pairing is the joke of his career: a revolutionary socialist who handed the mainstream its favourite diagram for price stickiness.",
