      "story": "Paul Marlor Sweezy was born in New York in 1910 to a wealthy banking family, and the contrast between his background and his politics was a standing joke of his career. He was educated at Exeter, then Harvard, where he studied economics, and in the early 1930s he went to the London School of Economics, where Harold Laski and the atmosphere of the Depression turned him decisively toward the left - and toward Marx, whose Capital he read in the British Museum. He returned to Harvard for his doctorate, joined the faculty in 1938, and at twenty-seven produced the paper that would carry his name into every textbook: 'Demand Under Conditions of Oligopoly', published in the Journal of Political Economy in 1939. In the same year, on the other side of the Atlantic, the Oxford economists Robert Hall and Charles Hitch arrived at the same kinked demand curve independently - the one genuine two-firm simultaneous discovery of the price-stickiness literature.\n\nIn 1942 Sweezy published The Theory of Capitalist Development, a systematic, sympathetic restatement of Marx's economics in the language of a modern economist. It remains a classic of Marxist scholarship, and it is one of the ironies of the era that the man who wrote it was also the man mainstream economists could not do without for a diagram. He left Harvard in 1946 - he had never been given tenure, a decision the university later had cause to defend at length - and in 1949, with the historian Leo Huberman, he founded Monthly Review, the independent socialist journal he edited for the rest of his life. In 1954 the state of New Hampshire tried to investigate him over subversive activities; Sweezy refused to answer the questions, fought the case to the Supreme Court, and won in 1957, a landmark of academic freedom.\n\nHis most influential later work was Monopoly Capital, written with Paul Baran and published in 1966, which argued that giant firms, absorbing the economic surplus, tended toward stagnation - a book read across the New Left and around the world. Through Monthly Review and through Monopoly Capital, Sweezy became the most widely read Marxist economist writing in English in the United States, even as mainstream economics moved in the other direction. He died in 2004, editor of Monthly Review almost to the end, having spent fifty-five years making the case that capitalism was not the only possible organisation of production. The kinked demand curve, meanwhile, outlived every political quarrel that surrounded it and is still drawn, in almost every economics classroom, exactly as he drew it in 1939.",
      "mechanism": "The kinked demand curve explains price stickiness in markets dominated by a few firms, and its genius is that it is a diagram you can see in one glance. An oligopolist faces a demand curve that is not a smooth downward slope but a curve with a sharp corner - a kink - at the current price. Above that price, the curve is relatively elastic: if this firm alone raises its price, its rivals keep theirs unchanged, customers leave, and the firm loses a great deal of business. Below the price, the curve is relatively inelastic: if this firm cuts its price, its rivals match the cut immediately to protect their market share, so the firm gains almost nothing. Each firm, in short, expects rivals to ignore a rise and to follow a fall.\n\nBecause the demand curve changes slope so abruptly at the kink, the marginal revenue curve develops a vertical gap directly beneath it. The profit-maximising firm sets output where marginal revenue equals marginal cost - and here is the point - any marginal cost curve that passes through that vertical gap delivers exactly the same price and output. Costs can rise or fall within a band, demand can shift a little, and the firm has no reason to move its price. The kink, in other words, is a trap that keeps the price exactly where it was.\n\nThe theory explains an observed fact that pure models had failed to capture: prices in oligopolistic industries, from steel to automobiles to cigarettes, change rarely and reluctantly even when costs change. It does not pretend to explain how the original price was set - the kink has to be at the current price - only why it stays put. Critics, led by George Stigler, noted the theory is silent on where the price starts, and empirical tests of rival response were mixed. But as an explanation of why firms in concentrated markets treat the price as nearly sacred, the kinked curve has never been bettered for clarity, which is why it is still on the board.",
      "significance": "Sweezy's significance rests on a rare double achievement: he gave the mainstream its standard diagram for price rigidity, and he was simultaneously the most prominent Marxist economist in the United States. The kinked demand curve entered the textbooks within a decade and has never left them - every economics student meets it in the same semester as Cournot and Bertrand, as one of the three classic answers to the question of how few-firm markets behave. Alongside Cournot's quantity competition and Bertrand's price competition, Sweezy's asymmetric expectations complete the triptych.\n\nIts limits became part of the story too. George Stigler's 1978 attack argued the empirical evidence for asymmetric rival response was thin, and later work showed the curve explains rigidity only within narrow bands. But the question it answered - why don't prices move? - proved to be one of the most durable puzzles in economics, and the kinked curve remains the standard textbook response.\n\nThe larger significance is biographical and political. Sweezy was the economist who turned the discipline's own tools toward its critique, and the kinked demand curve was, in a sense, his Trojan horse: a device for showing that the market mechanism, left to a few giant firms, does not behave as the textbooks of perfect competition promised. Monopoly Capital and Monthly Review carried the same message to a far larger audience than any journal article. He never stopped arguing, from 1949 to his death in 2004, that the concentration of capital was the central fact of modern capitalism - a claim that mainstream economics, in its own way, came around to agreeing with.",
