      "location": "Baghdad",
      "summary": "In September 1960, representatives of Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia and Venezuela met in Baghdad and founded the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries — the most successful cartel in history, and the only one with its own headquarters and flag. OPEC lives because oil is cheap to pump and hard to replace, so coordination is worth more than cheating.\n\nOver six decades it has staged embargoes, engineered two dramatic price shocks in the 1970s, and swung from impotence in the 1980s' glut back to quiet dominance. For economists it is the perfect live test of every model they teach about collusion, quotas and the temptation to cheat — and proof that a cartel can survive when its members are patient.\n",
