      "sim": {
        "id": "price-war",
        "label": "Simulation · slash prices, watch the floor",
        "caption": "Cut the price and your rival cuts back. Who blinks first?",
        "vector": {
          "decision": 0.7,
          "chance": 0.8,
          "history": 0.7
        }
      },
      "story": "In September 1960, in Baghdad, the state minister for oil affairs of Iraq hosted delegates from Iran, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, and Venezuela. The five governments had the same grievance: the international oil companies, the so-called Seven Sisters, had just cut the posted prices against which government revenues were calculated, slashing the income of producer states in the teeth of a glut. In a week of meetings the delegates founded the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries, OPEC, with headquarters in Geneva and, later, Vienna. It began, modestly enough, as a cartel of exporting states with a common front against the companies; it ended as the most successful cartel in history and the only one with its own flag.\n\nThe hatred that produced it was well voiced by Venezuela's minister of mines, Juan Pablo Pérez Alfonzo, who had campaigned for years for production quotas and who dismissed oil in a famous phrase as the excrement of the devil, devils excrement, a curse he believed the producing nations had invited upon themselves. Saudi Arabia's Sheikh Zaki Yamani, who became the cartel's most famous oil minister, put the eventual shift of power with equal bluntness: the Stone Age came to an end not for a lack of stones, and the Oil Age will come to an end not for a lack of oil.\n\nThe first sixteen years were a study in quiet build-up. OPEC set quotas, created a Secretariat, and slowly nationalised production. Then came 1973, the embargo against the countries supporting Israel, a quadrupling of the price of crude to more than eleven dollars a barrel, losing someone and everyone at once. The price shocks of the 1970s made OPEC the emblem of the world economy's new fragility, and the two decades that followed, a glut, collapsing discipline, and a price war in 1986, taught the cartel the corollary: coordination is worth more than cheating only while members are patient, and patience is finite. Expanded in 2016 into OPEC+, with Russia at the table, the cartel had, by its sixth decade, become the cartel the textbooks cannot ignore.",
      "mechanism": "OPEC works because oil is cheap to pump and hard to replace. Extraction costs in the great Persian Gulf fields are a few dollars a barrel, so a coordinated reduction in output that raises the price from, say, sixty to eighty dollars buys enormous revenue gains while accepting only modest lost volume. Because oil has no close substitute in transport and industry in the short run, demand does not flee when price rises; the cartel can exercise the elasticity of its members' collective restraint. Each member produces under a quota, the quotas being negotiated in Vienna, and the group as a whole behaves like a dominant supplier with a residual demand curve, shrinking output, leaving some demand unmet, and pocketing the higher price.\n\nThe structural problem is the prisoner's dilemma at the cartel's heart. If everyone honours the quota, the price stays high and revenue is shared; if any single member secretly pumps more, it captures the price for its extra barrels within the cooperation of the others. The temptation to cheat is permanent, and OPEC's history oscillates between cohesion and collapse as that temptation is resisted or yielded to. In the 1970s, with spare capacity concentrated among members and demand rising, cheating was unattractive and the cartel held. In the 1980s, with new suppliers, Saudi, American, and North Sea barrels flooding the market, the quota is a coordination device only as strong as the patience of its least patient member. When Saudi Arabia finally grew tired of cutting alone, it flooded the market in 1986 and prices collapsed from around thirty dollars to below ten, a demonstration that the mechanism runs equally well in reverse.",
