      "location": "Garmisch, Germany",
      "summary": "The discipline of building software that outlives the afternoon. Software engineering was named in 1968, at a NATO conference called in panic: the \"software crisis\" — programs that overran schedules, crashed in the field, and could not be maintained by anyone but their authors — had become too expensive to ignore. Out of that meeting came the field's founding question: how do you build large systems the way you build bridges, with engineering discipline rather than heroic effort.\n\nHalf a century later the answer is a stack of crafts — requirements, architecture, version control, testing, code review, deployment — each a small discipline in its own right, and none of them optional on any project that expects to run for more than a year.",
