      "summary": "The archive of every change a program has ever seen. Version control keeps the full history of a codebase — who changed what, when, and why — so any state can be recovered, any change examined, and any mistake rolled back. The earliest tools of the 1970s tracked single files; the modern generation, led by Git, tracks whole projects as a directed graph of versions.\n\nIt is the discipline's memory, and the quiet reason large teams can work together at all: without it, ten programmers editing one program is a game of telephone with no recording.",
