      "type": "concept",
      "label": "Agile Development",
      "tag": "Software Engineering",
      "dates": [
        2001
      ],
      "location": "Snowbird, Utah",
      "summary": "A way of building software that treats change as the normal condition. The Agile Manifesto, signed in 2001 at a ski resort in Utah by seventeen programmers, prefers individuals and interactions over processes, working software over documentation, customer collaboration over contract negotiation, and responding to change over following a plan. It is less a methodology than a permission slip.\n\nAgile's practice families — Scrum's sprints, Kanban's flow, Extreme Programming's testing and pairing — all share one engine: deliver small pieces of working software often, learn from what users actually do, and let the plan bend.",
