      "location": "MIT → the whole internet",
      "summary": "The belief that source code should be visible, shared, and improvable by anyone. The movement hardened out of the free-software ideals of the 1980s — Richard Stallman's GNU project and its copyleft licenses, which kept code free by licensing its derivatives free too — and rebranded as \"open source\" in 1998 when Netscape released its browser's code. The rest is the modern internet: Linux, Git, Python, the web itself.\n\nThe economic surprise is that it works. Tens of thousands of strangers coordinate on a single codebase with no boss and no pay, and produce the most widely used software in history.",
