      "summary": "The Finnish programmer who built the two most influential pieces of open-source software of the era: Linux, the kernel at the heart of the modern internet, and Git, the version-control system that organises almost all collaborative coding. He released the first Linux in 1991 at twenty-one with a Usenet post that called it \"just a hobby\".\n\nHis style — blunt, caustic, and exacting in review — made him the discipline's most famous gatekeeper, and his model of running a huge project by distributed trust, constant review, and a single decisive final say proved that open source could scale past every expectation.",
