      "story": "Science traces to the Latin verb scire, to know, and its noun scientia, knowledge. Behind the Latin there is a Proto-Indo-European root, *skei-, whose literal business was cutting: to know something meant, almost physically, to separate it from the surrounding noise, to discern one thing from another. The same root split off into scissors, schism and sheath. In that deep sense, science is the art of telling things apart, and English readers can feel the old meaning in religion's 'conscientia' and in the word conscious, which shares the stem.\n\nWhen 'sciens' or 'science' entered Middle English around 1300, it meant no more than the state of knowing, or what was known by study. In medieval universities scientia was any demonstrable and teachable body of knowledge, theology and music included; 'natural philosophy' was the particular business of studying the physical world, and the word science itself pointed at the whole tree, not one branch. It was only in the seventeenth century, as Francis Bacon's generation made experiment and induction the badge of reliable knowledge, that science began defaulting to the natural sciences, and by the nineteenth century the narrowing was complete.\n\nThe final turn came in 1834. William Whewell, at a meeting of the British Association, proposed a name for the cultivators of this new systematic knowledge: 'We need very much a name to describe a cultivator of science in general. I should incline to call him a Scientist.' The word spread reluctantly at first - Samuel Taylor Coleridge had floated 'scientist' on similar grounds a year earlier - but it stuck. What had always meant just 'knowing' had become a label for a profession and a method. The etymology is worth keeping in mind whenever anyone insists that such a word names only the natural sciences: the root still thinks of knowledge as a single, cuttable, clarifying act.",
      "mechanism": "The word assembles from scire 'to know' plus the agent suffix -ent-, giving sciens 'knowing', then the abstract-noun ending -ia, giving scientia 'the state of knowing'. Classical Latin scīre was itself probably formed from the idea of separating or distinguishing, and English took the whole noun through Old French science. The modern spelling has kept the Latin consonant cluster intact even where pronunciation has worn it smooth.\n\nThe meaning changed by narrowing, not by invention. A general term for knowledge gradually specialised to 'knowledge obtained by observation and tested by experiment', and then the practitioner's name, scientist, was coined in 1834 to match. British English quietly kept both the ancient breadth, as in 'moral science' or 'domestic science', and the modern restriction, as in physics, chemistry and biology - a single word split between two senses, like a sedimentary layer with a fault running through it.",
      "significance": "The etymology shows that a discipline did not come with its name; the name came first and the discipline came later to fill it. For most of Western history 'science' could be a theology, a music theory or a cookbook, which is a useful corrective whenever modern science is treated as timeless truth rather than a particular, recent way of ordering knowledge. The word also records a social transformation: when Whewell had to invent a name for people who did this work, it was a sign that experiment had become a profession with members, salaries and procedures.\n\nCross-references live naturally here. The mathematics tradition feeds directly into natural science, so the story of word-math anchors the word-science narrowings. And the scientist in the modern sense is exactly the practitioner the atomists' successors became - think of the counting at Ernest Rutherford's gold-foil apparatus, where 'science' meant a dark room, a zinc-sulfide screen, and a researcher of trained and separating attention.",
