      "story": "Mathematics is a Greek word that did not start as mathematics. Its root, manthanein, meant simply to learn, and mathema was originally 'that which is learnt' - a lesson, a piece of learning, a study. The Pythagoreans narrowed it a little, folding mathemata into the order of the quadrivium, and the Greek phrase he mathematike tekhne, 'the learning that is an art', came to mean the study of measurable order: number, space, music, the heavens. The English word inherited that whole aura of disciplined learning.\n\nThe path ran through Latin mathematica and Old French mathematique into English, where 'mathematic' appears by the late fourteenth century. The plural form mathematics, the one we use now, was established by the 1580s, modelled on physics and metaphysics; it is plural in shape and singular in use, and from it came the twentieth century's clipped labels, maths in Britain and math in America. An odd detour matters here: in Latin and in English until around 1700, a 'mathematician' was as likely to mean an astrologer, because the study of the heavenly motions was the most practical mathematics the universities knew. St Augustine's warning against the 'mathematici', meaning astrologers, is frequently miscopied as a warning against mathematicians entirely.\n\nSo the word mathematics is a lesson in humility. The most exact of sciences has a name that means neither numbers nor equations, but merely the general business of learning something properly. When a Roman said 'mathematica' he might have meant the calendar; when an early modern reader met 'mathematician' he might have met a stargazer. Only gradually, from about 1500 to 1800, did learning-general become number-and-space-specific, leaving the word slightly embarrassed, wearing a borrowed plural and an astrological past, while doing the most rigorous thing a culture can do with the mind.",
      "mechanism": "Mathematics is built from manthanein 'to learn' through mathema 'something learnt' to mathematikos 'of, or disposed to, learning', then the Greek plural ta mathematika, 'the things that are learnt', via Latin mathematica and French mathematique. English kept the plural spelling but treats the noun as singular, exactly as it does physics, metaphysics and acoustics - a grammatical fossil that reveals the Greek origins of the suffix.\n\nThe concept, not just the word, has a social mechanism: a study only counts as mathematical when it deals in order that can be demonstrated and repeated. The quadrivium selected music and astronomy because their ratios could be counted. Today the word has finished migrating, so a disgruntled student who says 'maths is learned, not discovered' is unknowingly rediscovering the ancient, etymological truth.",
      "significance": "The word mathematics shows how a science earned a name long before it earned its modern meaning. Its root insists that mathematics is about learning itself, order that can be taught, and its plural form records the Greek habit of thinking of the subject not as one thing but as a family of learned arts. That is why the library treats mathematics as a common base under algebra, arithmetic and the whole calculus family: the name was always plural, pointing at many disciplines at once.\n\nThe relief is that the word is honest. Every formula in word-logarithm, word-equation, word-calculus or word-prime is, etymologically, something 'learnt' - and the learning runs both ways, from Indian numeration to algebraic symbols to the calculus. Even the astrological detour is a reminder that mathematics has always been judged by usefulness first, mysticism second, and that the religion of numbers has usually been tolerated because the calculation pays for itself.",
