      "story": "Function comes from Latin fungi, 'to perform, discharge a duty', and functio, 'a performance, an execution'. Latin speakers said fungire munere, to perform one's office, and English borrowed the notion in 1530 as 'one's proper work or purpose, the power of acting in a specific way'. A body part functioned; an official discharged a function. It is entirely a word about employment and obligation, with only a distant whisper of anything mathematical in it.\n\nInside mathematics the word was set to work by Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz, who was forever renaming the tools of his new calculus. In August 1673 he wrote of 'functions' of a curve - other kinds of lines that, in a given figure, perform some role, such as the tangent or the normal. The term travelled through his reports in the Acta Eruditorum of 1692 and 1694, where he defined a function as a part of a straight line cut off by lines drawn from a fixed point - a definition so geometrical that it still shows the original 'office' metaphor. Johann Bernoulli tightened the idea in a letter of 1694, calling a function 'a quantity somehow formed from indeterminate and constant quantities', and the modern concept, a quantity depending on other quantities, grew through the eighteenth century with Euler, who gave it the notation f(x).\n\nThe English word arrived through seventeenth-century French fonction, and the mathematical sense was well established by the time Newton's rivals and heirs were writing. In our own time the word has done a second pass through computing: since 1947 'function' has meant a named routine that performs a task, taking inputs and returning outputs. So the etymology has held steady across three hundred years - a function, whether of a curve, of a variable, or of a program, is literally a well-defined unit whose job it is to do one thing.",
      "mechanism": "Fungere gives the past participle funct-, and functio adds the -ion suffix that changes an action into a thing done: so a function is etymologically 'something performed'. Latin fungi itself may go back to a root meaning to enjoy or benefit, which is why it is related to defunct, 'finished with office', off-duty, dead. The mathematics inherits the employment metaphor wholesale: saying y = f(x) is really saying that y performs an office on the number x - y is the output of a named job that the rule f does for every input.\n\nConcretely, take f(x) = x squared. On the payroll, the rule f is an employee who receives any input x and returns its square: f(2) = 4, f(10) = 100. The graph of f is a curve, and its tangent at each point is a 'function' in Leibniz's original sense, a line that performs a specific office for a given figure. Euler's notation, gradually standardised in the 1730s, turned the metaphor into machinery, and every modern programming language still uses the word to mean exactly this - a block of code that performs a defined duty.",
      "significance": "Function is the concept that made modern mathematics speak one language. Before it, mathematicians tracked curves individually; after Leibniz and Bernoulli, they had a single word for dependence - a quantity that changes with another - and calculus became the study of functions rather than a bundle of separate recipes. The word-equation and word-calculus entries lean on function everywhere, and the derivative of word-derivative is defined for functions, not for numbers. Etymology here is not decoration: to understand a function is to understand that something is performing an office on something else.\n\nIt is also the concept through which mathematics entered philosophy and finance. Economists speak of demand functions, physicists of wave functions, programmers of pure functions, and the word is still doing its original Latin job - a performance, an execution, a duty discharged. Whenever a spreadsheet cell reads '=F(C2)' the ancient verbs fungi and functio are still running, quietly insisting that a computation is an act of service performed by one quantity on another.",
