      "story": "The theorem outlives its name. Long before Pythagoras of Samos (c. 570-495 BC) was born, Babylonian scribes were writing down right-triangle facts. The clay tablet Plimpton 322, dated to around 1800 BC, lists whole-number triples, sets like 3, 4, 5 where the square of the largest equals the sum of the squares of the other two. The Egyptians, rebuilding boundaries after the Nile's floods, used a rope knotted in the ratio 3-4-5 to stake out right angles, and the Indian Sulba Sutras, composed between 800 and 500 BC, state the rule for laying out altars. The theorem was known before anyone thought of proving it; it was a fact of surveying before it was a fact of mathematics.\n\nPythagoras gave it a cult. His brotherhood at Croton, in southern Italy, taught that number was the essence of reality, and its members cherished proofs, not just recipes. Later writers, including the fifth-century commentator Proclus, report that the Pythagoreans attributed the theorem to their master and, on its discovery, sacrificed an ox. Historians are less sure what exactly Pythagoras proved; the story is better evidence of the school's devotion than of any single discovery.\n\nEuclid made it bulletproof. In the Elements, composed at Alexandria around 300 BC, Book I ends with Proposition 47: in right-angled triangles the square on the side subtending the right angle is equal to the squares on the sides containing the right angle. The proof proceeds by the careful machinery of the earlier propositions, and Proclus, generous to Pythagoras, reserved his greater admiration for Euclid's very lucid proof. For two thousand years this was the theorem's home address.\n\nThe theorem then revealed an uncomfortable secret. A right triangle with both legs of length 1 has a hypotenuse equal to the square root of 2, and the Pythagoreans found, so the legend runs, that this length cannot be written as a ratio of whole numbers. Proclus records that the man who publicised the discovery perished in a shipwreck, so that the inexpressible and unimaginable should ever remain veiled. Number, the essence of reality, was incomplete: some lengths refuse to be numbers in the ordinary sense.\n\nThe theorem's afterlife is everywhere. It is the foundation of Trigonometry, whose ratios live inside right triangles; it becomes the distance formula of coordinate geometry; and it is the seed of the modern idea of a metric, the notion of distance on which Geometry and even general relativity depend. Fermat's Last Theorem, that no integer powers beyond squares obey the same rule, is its haunting three-century-old cousin, settled by Andrew Wiles in 1995. The oldest result in mathematics is still the first thing every student proves.",
      "mechanism": "The theorem is easiest met with numbers. Take a right triangle whose legs are 3 and 4. Draw a square on each leg and on the hypotenuse. The square on the leg of length 3 has area 9; the square on 4 has area 16. The theorem says these two together equal the area of the square on the hypotenuse, so that square has area 25, and its side is 5. Hence 9 plus 16 equals 25, and the hypotenuse is 5. This is the theorem's most famous servant, the 3-4-5 triangle.\n\nThe general statement is the same with letters. If a right triangle has legs a and b and hypotenuse c, then a-squared plus b-squared equals c-squared. The hypotenuse, the side opposite the right angle, is always the longest side, so c-squared is the biggest of the three squares; the theorem says it is exactly as big as the other two put together. Square on the side can be read literally: the area argument is about physical squares, and it is why the proposition is stated in the language of squares.\n\nEuclid's proof in Book I, Proposition 47, shows the equality by cutting the square on the hypotenuse into two rectangles, then proving by congruent triangles that each rectangle matches one of the squares on the legs in area. Simpler dissections exist: cut the two smaller squares along suitable lines and the pieces rearrange to tile the large one. The point of a proof, for Euclid, is that the equality is forced by reasoning rather than by measuring; a measurement can only show one triangle, a proof shows all of them.\n\nThe theorem works backwards too, and that is the surveyor's trick. If the three sides of a triangle satisfy a-squared plus b-squared equals c-squared, the angle opposite c is a right angle. A rope with knots at 3, 4 and 5 units, stretched into a triangle, yields a perfect corner; the harpedonaptai, the Egyptian rope-stretchers, built right angles this way. Modern descendants include the distance formula of coordinate geometry, where the separation between two points is the hypotenuse of a right triangle, and the whole idea of measuring by perpendicular steps.",
      "significance": "The Pythagorean theorem is the archetype of a mathematical proof, the first theorem most people ever prove, and the standard against which proof is defined. It shows what Geometry is for: a statement about all right triangles, earned by reasoning and not by measurement, stands firm for two thousand years. When Euclid built his Elements, he ended the first book with it, a deliberate climax: the book that taught the world what proved means is anchored on a theorem that began as a rope trick.\n\nIt is also the founding stone of the mathematics of measurement. Trigonometry lives inside right triangles, its sine, cosine and tangent are ratios of the theorem's sides, so every height, distance and angle ever measured is an echo of a-squared plus b-squared equals c-squared. Coordinate geometry reads the theorem as the distance formula, and the modern idea of a metric, the way Geometry measures nearness and the way general relativity measures spacetime, is the theorem generalised.\n\nThe theorem's shadow is the discovery that some lengths are not ratios of whole numbers. The hypotenuse of a 1-by-1 right triangle, the square root of 2, is irrational, and the legend that its discoverer was drowned is the oldest warning that mathematics contains truths that offend intuition. That same incommensurability seeded the rigorous theory of real numbers, and its distant cousin Fermat's Last Theorem kept number theory busy for three and a half centuries until Andrew Wiles proved it in 1995. From a right triangle to the foundations of analysis and the summit of number theory, all in one equality.",
