      "story": "That one thing depends on another is old. Tables of squares, of shadows, of chords for every angle, all of these are functions in everything but name. What was missing was the name, and the realisation that dependence itself deserved study. It arrived in 1673, when Gottfried Leibniz used the word function for quantities connected to a curve: the ordinates, the tangents, the normals, each performing its office with respect to the curve. He was so pleased when Johann Bernoulli adopted the term that he wrote to say so.\n\nThe definition then relaxed by stages. Johann Bernoulli, in a 1694 letter to Leibniz, called a function a quantity somehow formed from indeterminate and constant quantities. Euler tightened the notation, introducing f(x) in 1734, and in his Introductio (1748) declared a function to be an analytic expression composed in any way whatsoever of the variable quantity and numbers or constant quantities. Still, a function meant a formula. Dirichlet in 1837 delivered the modern idea: a function is any rule that assigns to each input a unique output, whether the rule is a formula or not. His own example, the function that is 1 at rational numbers and 0 at irrational numbers, is calculated nowhere and defined everywhere.\n\nSet theory made it official. By the time Bourbaki formalised mathematics in the 1930s, a function was a mapping between sets: a relation in which each input has exactly one partner. Frege had already insisted, decades earlier, that a function was a complete thing in itself, as much a function as x-squared, an insight that gave logic its machinery.\n\nThen the idea escaped into machines. In computing, the function is the unit of procedure: a named block that takes inputs and returns outputs, the spine of every programming language. Alonzo Church's lambda calculus of 1936 made functions themselves the objects of computation, functions that take functions as arguments and return functions as results, and from it grew the functional programming languages that still carry the name. Euler's f and the lambda are the same move: treat the machine of dependence as a thing. The concept of a function is the shared grammar of Algebra, Trigonometry, The Calculus, Algorithms, and Programming Languages, and it is hard to say which field can claim it.",
      "mechanism": "A function is a rule with two rules of its own: one input, one output, and every allowed input gets an output. Think of a machine with a slot. Feed in 3 and the machine named square returns 9; feed in 4, it returns 16; feed in minus 3, it returns 9 again. Two different inputs may share an output, that is allowed, but a single input can never yield two outputs; the moment it does, the machine is not a function. The notation is Euler's: f(x) = x-squared names the machine, the letter x is the slot, and f(3) = 9 records a single working.\n\nThe machine has a visible face. Feed it the whole set of allowed inputs, the domain, and the outputs form the range. Plot the pairs (x, f(x)) on a plane, and the points trace the graph, the shape of the machine. For f(x) = x-squared, the graph is the parabola: it is defined for every real x, but its outputs are never negative, so the range is the non-negative numbers. The domain is part of the definition; change it and you change the function, even if the formula looks the same.\n\nFunctions also connect to one another. They compose: f(g(x)) means feed x to g, then feed g's output to f. They nest inside the calculus, which studies functions rather than plain numbers: the derivative is a machine that eats a function and returns its slope. And in computing the same idea wears a different coat: a function is a named procedure with parameters and a return value, and in modern languages functions are first-class objects, passed around like numbers. The lambda calculus of 1936 made the rule itself the thing under study, and functional programming built on that. The machine, it turns out, is the point.",
      "significance": "The function is the subject of the calculus and the verb of mathematics. Before it, y depends on x was a gesture; after Euler and Dirichlet it is an object that can be studied, differentiated, integrated, and compared. Most of modern mathematics is a conversation about functions: the calculus is the science of functions and their rates, Algebra studies classes of functions, and Trigonometry supplied the first genuinely important examples.\n\nIn the sciences a function is a model, position as a function of time, temperature as a function of position, demand as a function of price. Every physical law is an equation relating functions and their derivatives, which is why the function concept and the calculus grew up together and why The Calculus is the language in which the laws are written. In economics, marginal cost and marginal revenue are functions of quantity; the optimisation of every firm is a question about functions.\n\nIts most surprising career is in computation. The function is the atom of program structure, the named procedure with inputs and outputs, and the lambda calculus of 1936 showed that functions alone are enough to express all computation. Functional programming languages, first-class functions, and the theory of Algorithms all descend from the same move Euler made with f(x): treat the rule as a thing. No other mathematical object straddles the sciences, the humanities, and the machine quite so completely.",
