      "story": "We feed the nation into the machine card by card, and the machine never forgets and never blinks. The room where I work is called, without irony, the Tabulating Room, and it is the nearest thing a government has to a verdict. I am the clerk who reads the cards before they go in; I am not supposed to read them, I am supposed to check them, but after ten years the two things have become the same. I have learned to see a whole life in forty-five holes: a birth, a trade, a marriage, a death, an address, a column of figures that says what a man is worth and what he owes. It is an intimate business, tabulation. We simply conduct it with the door closed.\n\nThe card is a piece of stiff paper, and it is also a fact. That is the trick of the whole profession. A hole punched in the third position of the twelfth column is not a hole; it is a man's age, or his occupation, or his district, depending on how the panel is wired, and the machine believes it completely. You cannot argue with a card. You cannot persuade it, you cannot tell it your circumstances have changed, you cannot ask it to see things from your point of view. It is a fact, and the machine reads it the way a judge reads a statute, which is to say without mercy and without error.\n\nWe count a nation. The census of 1890, they say, took the old way eight years and the machines less than three, and since then no government in its right mind has gone back to the pens. So the whole of the country files past my table in the form of cards, and the machines, six of them, tall and black and patient, each with its great pool of mercury and its forest of pins, stand in a row like six well-behaved butlers. Opposite them the women who feed them stand in a row, one card at a time, hour after hour. The counters click. The sorted cards fall into their pockets. And at the end of the week a report climbs out of the machine that no one in the building could have written and everyone in the building must sign.\n\nI will tell you what unsettles me, and it is not the machine. The machine is honest in the way that arithmetic is honest. What unsettles me is that the machine never forgets, and I am paid to forget. I am the one who has been taught to look away. I check the card, I do not keep it. The machine keeps everything. It holds the whole country in a set of drawers, and when the Minister wants a number, the Minister does not ask the people; the Minister asks the drawers. We have taught the machine to remember so that we may be permitted to forget. I have decided, after long consideration, that this is either the triumph of the age or a bargain we will one day regret, and that the two things are probably the same.",
      "mechanism": "A punched-card tabulator is an electrical filing clerk. The card carries its data in the pattern of its holes: a card is divided into columns, each column one field, and the position of a hole within a column is the value, so a hole punched in one row makes the card say 4, and in another row the same column says 40. The card passes over a bed of small pins, one pin for each possible hole. Where the card is perforated, the pin drops through and dips into a pool of mercury, closing an electrical circuit; where the card is solid, the pin is blocked and nothing happens. Each completed circuit advances a counter, so as a hundred cards run past, the machine accumulates totals: the number of men in a district, the number of widows, the number of hands employed in a trade.\n\nThe tabulator only adds; the sorter is what divides. A sorter reads one column at a time and drops each card into one of twelve pockets according to the position of its hole, men here, women there, each age group into its own pocket, until the whole nation is filed by whatever column the clerk chooses. To count combinations, a weaver who is also a widow, say, the operator wires a control panel, plugging jacks so that two counters run only when both conditions are true at once. It is a logic of relays and mercury and paper, which is why the machine can be trusted with sixty million facts without blinking once. A human ledger-keeper must balance each page against the last; the tabulating room balances a whole country against itself, and the machine, unlike the clerk, does not grow tired at four in the afternoon.",
      "significance": "The Tabulating Room is the archive's witness to the decades when the word computer still meant a person and the machine had another name. Its clerks, the women feeding cards and the men wiring panels, were the unrecognised workforce behind every great count of the early twentieth century: the census, the draft, the insurance ledger, the payroll, the actuarial table. The room is where data first became a mass industrial product, produced by a small army of hands feeding stiff paper into machines that could not be argued with. When ENIAC's builders rewired their own machine in the 1940s, and when the stored-program computer made its programs live in memory rather than in card decks, they were standing on the tabulating room's shoulders.\n\nIts deeper significance is moral, and it is the same point Joseph Marr makes about the ledger and Clara Bennett about the thinking machines: a device that cannot be argued with cannot be forgiven. The card remembered what the clerk was trained to forget. The same Hollerith lineage, the company renamed IBM in 1924, later supplied tabulating machinery to the German censuses of the 1930s and to the administration of the camps, a documented chapter of punch-card history that no honest telling of the machine can skip. The room teaches the archive a lesson that runs all the way to the personal computer: counting people is the first step either to serving them or to sorting them, and the machine has never once expressed an opinion about which is which. Only the people around it ever choose, and they always had to be taught to look away.",
